// Every scenario below is authored teaching evidence, not a live measurement.
const S=(title,text,code,annotations=[])=>({title,text,evidence:{label:'INSPECT THE EXAMPLE',before:true,code,annotations}});
const Q=(kind,a,b,answer,checks)=>({kind,prompts:[a,b],answer,checks});
const C=(summary,sections,questions,example,trap)=>{sections[0].evidence.caption='Authored worked example, not a live capture. Names, addresses, tokens and timings are illustrative.';return {summary,sections,questions,example,trap,trace:sections[0].evidence.code,reworked:2};};
export const caseLessons={
 ethernet:C('Your laptop sends a file to a printer. The switch has an empty forwarding table—so how can the first frame reach the printer?',[
 S('Deliver the first frame before you know the destination port',`A laptop on switch port 2 wants to print on a device attached to port 5. We start after the laptop has learned the printer’s MAC address; that address is already in the frame. The switch’s own table is empty, which is a different kind of missing information. It must decide where to forward the frame.

The arriving source address tells the switch that the laptop is reachable on port 2. It learns that fact immediately. It has not yet learned the printer’s location, so a typical switch floods this unknown unicast to other eligible ports in the same VLAN. The printer receives a copy and accepts its destination address. Other interfaces can discard copies not addressed to them. The first delivery can succeed before the switch has a complete map.`,
 'VLAN 10, switch table initially empty\nPort 2: laptop   MAC 02:00:00:00:00:10\nPort 5: printer  MAC 02:00:00:00:00:50\nIncoming frame: src …:10, dst …:50', [['Source …:10','Learn laptop …:10 → incoming port 2.'],['Unknown destination …:50','Flood within VLAN 10, excluding the incoming port.']]),
 S('Use the reply to stop flooding',`The printer’s reply arrives on port 5. That source address teaches the switch where the printer is. The reply’s destination is the laptop, which the switch already learned on port 2, so this reply can be sent selectively.

A later laptop-to-printer frame can now be forwarded only toward port 5. The switch learned by observing source addresses, not by asking each endpoint to register. Entries can age out or move, so a forwarding table is maintained state rather than an eternal address book. Notice that this table maps MAC addresses to switch ports; the laptop’s neighbor cache maps IP addresses to link addresses. Confusing those two tables makes ordinary troubleshooting surprisingly difficult.`,
 'After laptop frame: …:10 → port 2\nAfter printer reply: …:50 → port 5\nNext known unicast …:10 → …:50: forward to port 5', [['Port 5 learned from source','The printer’s reply supplies the missing location.'],['Different caches','A switch forwarding table is not an ARP cache.']]),
 S('Follow the same print job from Wi-Fi',`Now unplug the laptop’s Ethernet cable and join Wi-Fi. Its first local transmission uses Wi-Fi frames to an access point. A typical home access point bridges this traffic onto the wired LAN where the printer lives. The wireless and wired frame formats need not be identical for the devices to exchange IP traffic.

Your home “router” may contain that access point, an Ethernet switch and a routing function in one box. A print job between interfaces in the same bridged LAN need not be routed out to the ISP. The box’s product name does not tell you which internal function handled a particular packet. Trace the actual local path before concluding that an Internet outage should prevent local printing.`,
 'Wi-Fi laptop → access point → LAN switch → printer\nLocal print job: no ISP hop is required\nSame enclosure may also contain router + DHCP + NAT', [['Access point','Connects wireless stations to the local network.'],['Switch versus router','Local bridging and between-network forwarding are different jobs.']]),
 S('Ask what happens at a broadcast boundary',`An ARP request to discover a local printer can be broadcast to its VLAN. The ordinary router at the edge does not simply repeat that Ethernet broadcast onto the Internet or another VLAN. That boundary contains local discovery traffic.

If the printer is instead in VLAN 20, merely connecting both VLANs to the same physical switch does not merge their broadcasts. Communication needs appropriate IP routing and policy, and some discovery mechanisms need additional arrangements. Finally, a switch seeing MAC …:50 proves that address arrived on a port; it does not cryptographically authenticate the printer. A malicious device can copy addresses. Location learning and trusted identity solve separate problems.`,
 'ARP broadcast in VLAN 10 → eligible VLAN 10 ports\nVLAN 20 → separate broadcast domain\nMAC learned on port 5 ≠ proof of an authorized printer', [['VLAN boundary','Limits the local broadcast domain.'],['Copied MAC','A forwarding identifier can be impersonated.']])
 ],[
 Q('Predict','The table is empty when the laptop frame arrives on port 2. What is learned, and how can the printer receive the frame?','Explain the first laptop-to-printer delivery using the empty switch table.', 'The switch learns the laptop’s source MAC on port 2. Because the printer’s unicast destination is unknown, it can flood to other eligible ports in VLAN 10. The printer receives and accepts the addressed frame. Its later reply lets the switch learn the printer on port 5; subsequent known unicast can be selective.',['Learn source …:10 on port 2','Unknown unicast can be flooded within VLAN 10','Printer reply teaches port 5']),
 Q('Explain','A Wi-Fi laptop prints to a wired printer while the ISP is down. Which jobs inside the home box can make that work?','Why need this Wi-Fi-to-printer exchange not travel through the Internet?', 'The access point bridges wireless connectivity to the LAN, and switching forwards frames toward the printer. Those local jobs can work while the WAN or ISP path fails. The same enclosure may also route, supply DHCP and translate addresses, but not every exchange uses every function.',['Access point bridges wireless to LAN','Switching provides local delivery','ISP path is not required for this local case']),
 Q('Security','Another device sends frames with the printer’s MAC. What has the switch learned, and what has it not proved?','Why does learning …:50 on a port not authenticate the printer?', 'It has learned a forwarding location for the presented source address. A MAC can be copied, so that observation alone does not establish authorized device identity. Authentication is needed for a stronger identity claim; an address-based allowlist does not create cryptographic proof.',['Forwarding location','Address can be copied','Authentication is separate'])
 ],['Observe the laptop source and learn port 2.','Flood the unknown printer destination within the VLAN.','Learn port 5 from the printer’s reply.','Use known destinations for selective later delivery.'], 'An empty switch table does not mean an empty laptop ARP cache. Keep MAC-to-port learning separate from IP-to-MAC resolution.'),
 subnets:C('A laptop at 192.168.1.70/26 can reach .120 but not .130 directly. Work out the boundary instead of guessing from the first three numbers.',[
 S('Find the block containing this laptop',`The /26 means the first 26 of the address’s 32 bits define this connected prefix. That leaves six address bits: 2^6 gives 64 addresses per block. In the last octet, the blocks begin at 0, 64, 128 and 192. Our laptop’s .70 falls in the block beginning at .64.

The connected range is therefore 192.168.1.64 through .127. In an ordinary IPv4 LAN, .64 names the network and .127 is its broadcast address; .65–.126 are conventional host addresses. This is why .120 and .130, despite looking similar, fall on different sides of this laptop’s local boundary. The prefix supplies the boundary; the dotted address by itself does not.`,
 'Laptop: 192.168.1.70/26\nMask:   255.255.255.192\nBlocks: .0–.63 | .64–.127 | .128–.191 | .192–.255', [['32 − 26 = 6','Six remaining bits give 64 addresses in a block.'],['Containing prefix','192.168.1.64/26 includes the laptop and .120.']]),
 S('Make the delivery decision using bits',`The operating system builds a connected route from the interface’s prefix. To reason about membership, compare the destination’s network bits with the laptop’s network bits. A bitwise AND with the subnet mask performs that comparison.

For .70, the last-octet bits are 01000110. The /26 mask’s final octet is 11000000. Their AND is 01000000, or 64. Address .120 produces the same 64, while .130 produces 128. Under these ordinary connected-route assumptions, .120 can be delivered through a local neighbor lookup; .130 requires another applicable route. A real routing table can contain more specific exceptions, so the bit calculation informs the route lookup rather than replacing it.`,
 '.70:  01000110 AND 11000000 = 01000000 (.64)\n.120: 01111000 AND 11000000 = 01000000 (.64)\n.130: 10000010 AND 11000000 = 10000000 (.128)', [['Same result','Addresses share this /26 network prefix.'],['Different result','The destination is outside that connected prefix.']]),
 S('Use the gateway without changing the final destination',`Suppose the routing table also has a default route via 192.168.1.65. To contact .130 with no more specific route, the laptop sends toward that local gateway. It resolves the gateway’s link address and builds a frame for it. The enclosed IP packet still has destination 192.168.1.130.

A default gateway is useful because the laptop cannot directly reach every remote network. It delegates onward delivery to a neighbor with routing capability. That does not guarantee success: the gateway needs an onward route, policy must allow the traffic, and replies need a usable return path. “Send to the gateway” is a forwarding decision, not an assertion that the destination exists or will answer.`,
 '192.168.1.64/26 dev LAN\ndefault via 192.168.1.65\nPacket for .130 → local frame to gateway .65\nIP destination remains 192.168.1.130', [['Next hop .65','A reachable neighbor inside the connected subnet.'],['Final IP .130','The endpoint being sought, not the gateway.']]),
 S('Diagnose the misleading “same switch” case',`A second host at .130 is plugged into the same switch but configured with /24. It considers .70 on-link, while the laptop at .70/26 considers .130 off-link. Their physical proximity has not made their IP decisions agree.

The laptop might send through its gateway while the other host tries direct resolution, and the return path or router policy may prevent a useful exchange. Inspect both masks and both routing tables rather than deciding that the switch is broken. Also avoid mechanically applying “subtract two host addresses” to every prefix: /31 point-to-point links and /32 host routes have special uses. The ordinary LAN arithmetic above is a worked case with stated assumptions.`,
 'Host A: .70/26 → .130 is outside connected prefix\nHost B: .130/24 → .70 is inside connected prefix\nSame Ethernet switch, inconsistent IP assumptions', [['Physical link','Does not define a shared IP prefix by itself.'],['Two directions','Each sender independently chooses a return or forward route.']])
 ],[
 Q('Calculate','For .70/26, show the network, ordinary broadcast and whether .120 and .130 share the prefix.','Calculate the /26 block around 192.168.1.70 and classify .120 versus .130.', 'Six host bits give blocks of 64. The containing range is .64–.127, with conventional network .64 and broadcast .127. Address .120 belongs to that prefix; .130 lies in the next block. The decision depends on /26, not merely the matching first three octets.',['64-address block','Network .64 and broadcast .127','.120 inside; .130 outside']),
 Q('Predict','Using the displayed default route via .65, which IP is resolved to a MAC for a packet to .130?','What are the next hop and final IP when .70/26 sends to .130 through gateway .65?', 'Resolve the selected local gateway 192.168.1.65 to a link address and send the frame there. The IP packet remains addressed to .130. This route is selected because .130 is outside the connected /26 and the example gives no more specific route.',['Outside connected /26','Resolve gateway .65','Keep destination .130']),
 Q('Diagnose','Why can .70/26 and .130/24 disagree about local delivery despite sharing a switch?','What would you inspect before blaming the switch for these two hosts’ failure?', 'Their configured prefixes lead to different on-link decisions. Inspect addresses, masks, routes and return-path policy on both sides. A common physical link does not correct inconsistent IP configuration, and the two hosts can choose different delivery paths.',['Masks change on-link decisions','Inspect both routing tables','Return path and policy matter'])
 ],['Subtract 26 from 32 to find six remaining bits.','Find the 64-address block containing .70.','Compare each destination under the same mask.','Use a matching route and local next hop for off-link traffic.'], 'A destination that looks nearby in dotted notation may be off-link. Calculate with the configured prefix and then inspect the actual route.'),
 arp:C('The laptop knows the web server’s IP but still cannot send its first Ethernet frame. Find the missing local address.',[
 S('Spot what is missing after the route lookup',`The browser has selected server 198.51.100.20. The laptop’s route says to forward remote traffic through gateway 192.168.1.1. That has answered which local neighbor should receive the packet, but the Ethernet interface still needs a destination MAC to build its frame.

The neighbor cache below has no mapping for the gateway. The missing fact is not the server’s name or IP; it is the gateway interface’s link address. ARP, Address Resolution Protocol, supplies that local IPv4-to-link-address mapping. If a usable mapping were already cached, this extra exchange could be skipped. Thinking through the empty entry gives ARP a reason to exist instead of another acronym to memorize.`,
 'Laptop: 192.168.1.10/24\nServer selected: 198.51.100.20\nRoute: default via 192.168.1.1\nNeighbor cache: 192.168.1.1 → ?',[['Route selection','The next hop is the local gateway.'],['Missing MAC','The interface still cannot address its outgoing Ethernet frame.']]),
 S('Ask locally and read the answer',`The laptop broadcasts a question asking who owns 192.168.1.1. Ethernet broadcast delivery lets eligible local interfaces hear it without the laptop knowing the owner’s individual MAC. The gateway normally replies directly, supplying its link address.

The laptop can record that answer and construct a unicast frame. This request does not travel through ordinary routers across the Internet. Asking “who owns the remote server’s address?” on this LAN would not normally discover an arbitrary distant machine. The ARP target follows the selected local next hop: the destination host if it is on-link, otherwise a gateway. That order—route, then local resolution—is the useful reasoning sequence.`,
 'Broadcast request: Who has 192.168.1.1? Tell .10\nGateway reply: 192.168.1.1 is at 02:00:00:00:00:01\nCache now:    192.168.1.1 → 02:00:00:00:00:01',[['Broadcast request','Reach the unknown owner on this local domain.'],['Reply mapping','Supply the MAC needed for a unicast frame.']]),
 S('Inspect the frame that can finally leave',`The resulting frame names the gateway MAC while its IP payload names the server. Those fields are not contradictory. The frame’s recipient can remove the local wrapper and forward the packet without being the application’s final endpoint.

A DNS answer, by contrast, supplies name-related data such as the selected server address. DNS would not supply the gateway’s Ethernet address. If DNS succeeds but the neighbor entry stays unresolved, the laptop can know where the application lives while still failing at the first local hop. Inspect the route and neighbor state to distinguish that failure from an unavailable web application. A valid answer at one layer cannot substitute for information required at another.`,
 'Ethernet dst: 02:00:00:00:00:01 (gateway)\nIP dst:       198.51.100.20 (web service)\nHTTP meaning: get the selected resource',[['DNS answer','Helps choose a network destination for a name.'],['ARP answer','Enables local frame delivery to a selected neighbor.']]),
 S('Notice what the reply has not proved',`Suppose another local device announces that gateway IP .1 belongs to its own MAC. Basic ARP contains no cryptographic proof that the claimant is the authorized gateway. Depending on host behavior and local defenses, an unwanted mapping can redirect or disrupt traffic.

This trust limit explains ARP poisoning without requiring an attack recipe. The forged claim concerns local delivery information. Properly validated HTTPS still demands the intended TLS identity; redirecting frames alone does not supply the server’s private key. In IPv6, local neighbor information uses a different protocol, Neighbor Discovery. Keep the task—finding local delivery information—separate from the particular IPv4 mechanism used here.`,
 'Claim A: .1 → 02:00:00:00:00:01\nUnwanted claim B: .1 → 02:00:00:00:00:99\nAccepting a mapping ≠ authenticating a gateway',[['Trust assumption','A basic ARP claim is not signed identity evidence.'],['Independent TLS check','Redirected traffic still faces server-identity validation.']])
 ],[
 Q('Trace','With this route and empty neighbor cache, explain the first Ethernet send to 198.51.100.20.','What happens between selecting gateway .1 and putting the remote packet on the LAN?', 'The laptop asks locally for .1’s MAC, receives the mapping, caches it and sends a frame to that MAC. Inside, the destination IP remains 198.51.100.20. It resolves the local gateway rather than attempting ARP for the distant server.',['Route selects .1','ARP resolves .1 locally','Remote IP stays in the packet']),
 Q('Predict','A later request succeeds but your capture shows no ARP. Give two ordinary explanations.','Why might this successful connection need no fresh ARP exchange?', 'A usable gateway mapping may already be cached, or resolution may have happened before the capture started. ARP is used when mapping information is needed or refreshed, not before every application request or IP packet. Inspect cache state and capture timing before treating the absence as a contradiction.',['Mapping reuse','Earlier exchange may be outside capture','ARP is not per-packet']),
 Q('Distinguish','DNS returned 198.51.100.20. Why can the empty entry for .1 still block the request?','Explain why a server IP answer cannot replace the missing gateway MAC.', 'The DNS result supplies a network destination associated with a name. The selected local Ethernet next hop still needs a link address. An unresolved gateway mapping can therefore stop first-hop delivery even after a successful lookup. These answers solve different dependencies.',['DNS selects name-related destination data','Frame needs local next-hop MAC','First hop can fail after DNS succeeds'])
 ],['Select the route to the server.','Check the mapping for its local next hop.','Resolve the gateway with a local ARP exchange if needed.','Send a frame to the gateway containing the remote IP packet.'], 'Do not ask ARP to solve a DNS problem or expect a successful DNS lookup to establish first-hop Ethernet delivery.'),
 dhcp:C('A newly joined laptop needs an address before browsing. Read the lease exchange, then diagnose why a successful lease can still leave websites unreachable.',[
 S('Join without already owning a usable IPv4 address',`A new laptop has joined the local link but has no ordinary IPv4 address to use. Its initial DHCP request can use source 0.0.0.0 and broadcast delivery. A nearby DHCP server or relay can receive it. That special starting mechanism avoids requiring the configuration the client is trying to obtain.

Read the four messages as negotiation: discover available service, receive an offer, request a selected offer, then receive an acknowledgement. A relay can carry DHCP messages to a server on another network; the client’s Ethernet broadcast does not itself cross ordinary routers. UDP server port 67 and client port 68 identify the protocol endpoints in this IPv4 exchange.`,
 'Client :68 → server :67  Discover\nServer → client         Offer 192.168.1.42\nClient → server         Request .42 from this server\nServer → client         Acknowledge lease + options',[['Discover','Find configuration service without an ordinary address.'],['Acknowledge','Confirms the lease and supplies applicable options.']]),
 S('Read the offer as separate pieces of configuration',`The offered .42 address is only one value. The mask controls the connected-prefix interpretation. The router option supplies a candidate default gateway. The DNS option identifies resolver addresses. The lease duration governs how long the client may use the assignment under the protocol’s rules.

If the resolver option names an unreachable machine, receiving .42 does not make name resolution work. If the router option is wrong, off-link connections can fail while some local communication still works. Inspect the options rather than concluding “DHCP worked, therefore the network works.” The server may provide these services on the same box, but configuration delivery and those services’ actual operation remain different jobs.`,
 'Address: 192.168.1.42\nMask:    255.255.255.0 (/24)\nRouter:  192.168.1.1\nDNS:     192.168.1.53\nLease:   3600 seconds',[['Mask','Defines the ordinary connected prefix.'],['DNS .53','A service the laptop must subsequently reach and query.']]),
 S('Renew a borrowed address rather than starting over every hour',`A 3600-second lease is not a promise that the address belongs to the laptop forever. A client normally attempts renewal before expiry. It can initially contact the existing server directly, so a renewal trace need not contain the same broadcast Discover/Offer sequence as a first lease.

If the server cannot be reached, the client’s behavior progresses according to lease timers and eventually it cannot keep treating an expired assignment as valid. A device can also be statically configured or use a fallback address after configuration fails. Seeing an automatically chosen address therefore does not by itself prove that this particular DHCP negotiation completed. Check lease state, not just whether a number is displayed beside the interface.`,
 'Initial lease: Discover → Offer → Request → Ack\nLater: client attempts renewal before expiry\nNo fresh Discover in a trace may be normal',[['Lease state','Explains whether an assignment remains valid.'],['Renewal','May be directed to the known server rather than repeating discovery.']]),
 S('Diagnose a connected laptop with the wrong resolver',`Suppose .42 can reach a known internal service, but querying the advertised resolver .53 produces no reply. That points you toward resolver availability, the path to it and relevant filtering. It does not prove that every external website has failed. A clean test must preserve the application’s requirements; replacing an HTTPS hostname with a bare IP can introduce certificate or virtual-host errors of its own.

Also ask whether the configuration source was authorized. An unwanted DHCP offer can supply a misleading router or resolver and influence later paths. Managed networks may restrict which switch ports can issue server replies. Validated TLS remains an independent identity check even when network configuration sends traffic through an unwanted route.`,
 'Lease received: .42/24\nInternal service reachable\nQuery to advertised DNS .53: no usable reply\nNext test: resolver/path/configuration, not every website',[['Lease success','Confirms configuration delivery, not resolver health.'],['Rogue offer','Can influence routing and resolution without breaking TLS by itself.']])
 ],[
 Q('Explain','How can this unconfigured laptop send its first DHCP request, and what follows?','Walk through obtaining .42 without assuming the client already has a usable IPv4 address.', 'It can use source 0.0.0.0 and local broadcast delivery to reach a DHCP server or relay. In the common initial sequence, Discover obtains an Offer, Request identifies the chosen offer and Acknowledge confirms the lease/options. Special initial delivery avoids the address-before-configuration problem.',['Special source and broadcast','Server or relay','Discover/Offer/Request/Acknowledge']),
 Q('Diagnose','The .42 lease succeeded, but the advertised resolver .53 does not answer. What would you inspect?','Why can this acknowledged lease coexist with failed name lookups?', 'The DNS option is separate from address assignment. Check whether .53 is the intended resolver, whether the laptop can reach it, and what response or timeout occurs. Also verify prefix and routes as dependencies. Receiving configuration is not a health check of every configured service.',['Inspect DNS option','Resolver/path response evidence','Separate configuration from service health']),
 Q('Security','An unwanted offer changes the router from .1 to .99. What influence does this give, and what does it not automatically defeat?','Explain the risk of accepting a rogue gateway or resolver option.', 'It can redirect or disrupt subsequent paths and name lookups. It does not automatically provide the private key or valid certificate needed to impersonate the intended HTTPS service. Check configuration-source authorization while retaining independent endpoint authentication.',['Influences path or DNS','Can disrupt or redirect','TLS validation still applies'])
 ],['Observe the initial special-address exchange.','Read address, prefix, gateway and resolver separately.','Track the lease and later renewal.','Test the advertised services rather than inferring their health.'], 'A successful DHCP acknowledgement means configuration arrived. It does not establish that the gateway, resolver or remote application is functioning.'),
 routing:C('A router has three matching routes for 198.51.100.150. Choose the actual next hop, then explain why ping and large transfers can tell different stories.',[
 S('Choose among routes that all seem to fit',`The destination .150 matches the /24 route, the .128/25 route and the default route. The most specific matching prefix is /25, so forwarding uses router B. A /25 fixes 25 leading bits; its .128 half covers .128–.255. A default route fixes no destination bits and serves as a catch-all.

This is longest prefix match, derived here from a concrete decision rather than a slogan. Administrative policy and metrics help select eligible routes, but once this forwarding table is given, specificity explains this lookup. Each router makes its own next-hop decision. The laptop does not need to embed the entire cross-Internet itinerary in the packet.`,
 '198.51.100.0/24   → router A\n198.51.100.128/25 → router B\n0.0.0.0/0        → router C\nDestination: 198.51.100.150',[['/25','The most specific of the three matching routes.'],['Default /0','Used when no more specific eligible prefix wins.']]),
 S('Follow a packet that would otherwise loop',`Imagine a routing mistake sends traffic in a circle. IPv4 TTL is decremented as routers forward the packet. Starting at 3, the packet can expire at a later hop instead of circulating indefinitely. A router discarding it for expiry may return ICMP Time Exceeded.

Traceroute uses deliberately small limits to obtain responses from progressively farther hops. A star in its output means that probe did not obtain the expected response within the wait period. A router might filter or rate-limit that response while continuing to forward other traffic. It is not a complete proof that a particular hop cannot route your web connection. Interpret the reply mechanism as well as the forward path.`,
 'Probe TTL 1 → first responding hop\nProbe TTL 2 → next responding hop\nProbe TTL 3 → * (no expected reply in time)',[['TTL','Limits forwarding; it is not a DNS cache lifetime.'],['*','Missing probe response, with several possible causes.']]),
 S('Fit the packet through a smaller link',`Suppose the endpoint sends a 1500-byte IPv4 packet, but a later link can carry only 1400 bytes at the IP level. With fragmentation prohibited for that packet, it cannot simply be forwarded unchanged. Relevant ICMP feedback can tell the sender that a smaller packet is needed.

If that feedback is lost or filtered, small packets may succeed while larger transfers repeatedly fail. That symptom motivates a path-MTU hypothesis; it does not uniquely prove it. IPv6 routers do not fragment forwarded packets and use Packet Too Big feedback as part of handling this constraint. MTU is about packet size on the path, whereas adding application workers would address an entirely different resource.`,
 'Sender packet: 1500 bytes\nRestrictive next link: MTU 1400\nIPv4 example: DF set → cannot fragment here\nNeeded: usable feedback or probing + smaller packets',[['DF','The IPv4 packet disallows this fragmentation.'],['1400','A link-size limit, not an application message limit.']]),
 S('Separate a probe result from application availability',`The same host may ignore ICMP echo yet serve HTTPS successfully, or answer ping while nothing listens on TCP 443. These observations can coexist because they exercise different protocols and policy. A reply also needs its own return route; it need not retrace the exact forward path.

For a browser failure, record which exchange actually succeeded: local neighbor delivery, an echo reply, a TCP opening, or an HTTP response. Then choose a test of the next dependency. “Ping is broken” and “the Internet is broken” are not equivalent conclusions. The table, TTL sequence and MTU example describe different mechanisms that can affect one user-visible failure, so avoid choosing one merely because its acronym is familiar.`,
 'Case A: ping timeout, HTTPS 200\nCase B: ping reply, TCP 443 reset\nSame host address; different tested services',[['Echo success','Evidence for that ICMP exchange.'],['Web success','Needs its own transport, security and application path.']])
 ],[
 Q('Calculate','Which next hop receives .150 under this forwarding table, and why?','Explain why router B wins despite two other matching routes.', 'Router B’s .128/25 is the longest matching prefix. Both /24 and /0 also match but specify fewer leading bits. The lookup selects a next hop from the given table; it does not guarantee that the destination or return path is healthy.',['.150 belongs to .128/25','/25 is more specific','Choose router B']),
 Q('Diagnose','This host times out on ping but returns HTTPS 200. Is the evidence inconsistent?','What would failed ICMP echo establish about TCP 443 in this example?', 'It is consistent. Echo and HTTPS use different protocol paths and filtering rules. Echo may be blocked or rate-limited while TCP/TLS/HTTP works. State the narrow result of each test instead of turning one missing reply into a universal outage verdict.',['Different exchanges','Filtering or rate limits','Bounded interpretation']),
 Q('Investigate','Small exchanges work but 1500-byte DF packets meet a 1400-byte link. What would you inspect?','Explain a path-MTU hypothesis for a connection that stalls only on larger transfers.', 'Inspect whether size feedback reaches the sender and whether probing or size adjustment finds a workable packet size. Small packets fit while larger ones can be dropped. Confirm the hypothesis with evidence; loss, application behavior and other causes can produce similar symptoms.',['Packet versus link limit','Feedback/size adjustment','Hypothesis needs confirmation'])
 ],['Match .150 against all candidate prefixes.','Choose /25 and router B.','Account for forwarding limits and link size separately.','Test the intended service and return path instead of treating ping as universal proof.'], 'Forwarding, ICMP response policy and application health are separate observations. One probe cannot prove all three.')
 ,nat:C('Two laptops open HTTPS connections using the same source port. See how one public IPv4 address still gets each reply to the right device.',[
 S('Give two identical-looking clients different public endpoints',`Laptop A and laptop B both choose source port 53000 when contacting the same server. Their private IP addresses distinguish them on the LAN. Outside the home network, the router translates their sources to one public address but assigns different public ports.

The two flows therefore remain distinguishable as .5:62001 and .5:62002. The server does not see the private addresses in the packets it receives under this translation arrangement. Network address and port translation lets several clients share an external IPv4 address; the useful question is which fields identify each flow on each side of the router.`,
 'A: 192.168.1.10:53000 → 198.51.100.20:443\nB: 192.168.1.11:53000 → 198.51.100.20:443\nA outside: 203.0.113.5:62001 → server:443\nB outside: 203.0.113.5:62002 → server:443',[['Private IPs','Distinguish A from B before translation.'],['Public ports','Distinguish these flows after sharing public address .5.']]),
 S('Use recorded state to translate a reply',`A reply addressed to .5:62001 is matched to the recorded mapping for laptop A. The router rewrites the relevant destination to 192.168.1.10:53000 and forwards internally. A reply for 62002 uses B’s mapping instead.

The router needs enough flow state and protocol handling to distinguish the traffic correctly. It is not guessing a laptop from the public IP alone. Mappings can expire, and later traffic can reuse addresses and ports. A public IP in a log therefore cannot by itself identify a unique device or person. You need the relevant times and additional records to interpret a shared address.`,
 'Translation state for this example:\n203.0.113.5:62001 ↔ 192.168.1.10:53000\n203.0.113.5:62002 ↔ 192.168.1.11:53000\nReply for :62001 → laptop A',[['State','Remembers the association needed for reverse translation.'],['Time','An old mapping is not a permanent identity.']]),
 S('Explain why a new inbound connection has no obvious recipient',`An unsolicited connection to .5:65000 may match neither mapping. Which internal host should receive it? The router needs a configured forwarding arrangement or another applicable mapping and policy; the public address alone supplies no answer.

Now place an ISP’s carrier-grade NAT in front of the home router. Your local port-forward rule configures only your device. The upstream translator may still have no inbound mapping toward you. Successful outbound browsing is compatible with failed unsolicited inbound access. Drawing both translation boundaries explains why editing one home setting may not solve the whole path.`,
 'Internet → ISP translator → home translator → laptop\nHome rule for :65000 exists\nISP inbound mapping: absent',[['Upstream boundary','Controlled separately from your home forwarding rule.'],['Outbound success','Can create state without providing general inbound reachability.']]),
 S('Separate changing addresses from deciding permission',`A home box commonly translates addresses and applies stateful filtering. Those jobs often produce the familiar experience that replies work while unsolicited connections fail. But translation is a rewriting mechanism; a firewall is traffic policy.

An IPv6 host can have a globally usable address and still reject unsolicited inbound flows through a firewall. Conversely, exposing a forwarding rule should be evaluated by its service and policy, not by assuming that NAT makes all internal software safe. Treat the observed mapping and the allow/deny decision as two separate facts when explaining a connection.`,
 'NAT: which addresses/ports are rewritten?\nFirewall: which traffic is allowed?\nGlobal IPv6 + restrictive firewall is a valid arrangement',[['Translation','Changes packet addressing.'],['Filtering','Enforces permission based on configured rules and state.']])
 ],[
 Q('Trace','A reply arrives at public port 62001. Explain its path to the correct laptop and port.','How does the router distinguish replies for A and B despite one public IP?', 'It matches the reply to recorded translation state. Port 62001 maps to A at 192.168.1.10:53000; 62002 maps to B in this example. Reverse translation restores the internal destination before forwarding. The common public IP alone cannot select a client.',['Recorded mapping','Restore internal address and port','Distinct public ports separate these flows']),
 Q('Diagnose','Why can the home forwarding rule for 65000 still fail behind the ISP translator?','Which missing arrangement explains outbound success but failed unsolicited inbound traffic here?', 'The upstream translator can lack a mapping toward the home router. A local rule cannot configure that separate device. The relevant translation and policy boundaries must all permit a usable path; outbound state does not establish general inbound access.',['Separate ISP translation','No upstream mapping','Local rule has limited scope']),
 Q('Distinguish','An IPv6 host has no address-sharing NAT. What decides whether a new inbound flow is allowed?','Explain why removing address translation does not remove the need for traffic policy.', 'A firewall or other policy determines allowed traffic. NAT rewrites addresses, whereas filtering permits or denies flows. Globally usable addressing can coexist with restrictive stateful policy, so the absence of translation does not mean that every service is exposed.',['Policy decides permission','Translation is distinct','Global address can have restrictive filtering'])
 ],['Identify each private flow.','Assign distinct translated endpoints.','Use the mapping for returning traffic.','Inspect every translation and filtering boundary for unsolicited inbound access.'], 'Sharing one public IP is not sharing one identity, and address rewriting is not a complete security policy.'),
 ipv6:C('A laptop learns an IPv6 prefix and a default router from a local advertisement. Follow how it can send without any ARP exchange.',[
 S('Read what the router actually advertises',`The router advertises a documentation prefix, a nonzero router lifetime and an autonomous-address-configuration flag. The lifetime says it can be considered for default-router use during the indicated period. The prefix information can help the host configure addresses and understand on-link information according to its flags.

A /64 leaves 64 address bits beyond the advertised prefix. Under suitable SLAAC rules, the host forms an address using that prefix and its local interface-identifier behavior. It also performs applicable checks such as duplicate-address detection. The router’s link-local address is useful locally even though it is not a globally routed destination. Read the advertisement’s functions separately instead of expecting one DHCP-like gateway field.`,
 'Router Advertisement from fe80::1\nRouter lifetime: 1800 s\nPrefix: 2001:db8:42:1::/64\nAutonomous flag A=1; on-link flag L=1',[['Router lifetime','Default-router availability information.'],['Prefix and flags','Inform address configuration and local-prefix behavior.']]),
 S('Resolve the next-hop link address without ARP',`To deliver to its local router, the laptop needs link information. IPv6 uses Neighbor Discovery, carried in ICMPv6, rather than ARP. A Neighbor Solicitation and Advertisement can learn or verify the relevant mapping.

Relevant discovery uses multicast rather than IPv4-style broadcast. Multicast identifies a group of interested interfaces instead of treating the entire link as an IPv4 broadcast domain. The high-level reasoning still resembles the IPv4 case: select a route and obtain local neighbor information as needed. The packet format and discovery protocol differ. A trace with IPv6 traffic and no ARP is therefore entirely normal, not evidence that local addressing was unnecessary.`,
 'Laptop → Neighbor Solicitation for fe80::1\nRouter → Neighbor Advertisement with link information\nLocal neighbor cache can now support forwarding',[['ICMPv6','Carries these neighbor-control messages.'],['No ARP','IPv6 solves this local task with Neighbor Discovery.']]),
 S('Keep SLAAC, DHCPv6 and DNS roles distinct',`SLAAC is Stateless Address Autoconfiguration: the host can form an address using advertised prefix information. DHCPv6 may provide addresses or additional configuration, depending on deployment. Default-router discovery normally comes from Router Advertisements rather than a DHCPv6 counterpart of the IPv4 router option.

When a hostname has A and AAAA records, a dual-stack client has IPv4 and IPv6 candidates. It can try suitable paths according to its connection strategy. An IPv4 success does not prove the IPv6 path works, and an AAAA record does not guarantee reachability. Inspect the address and protocol actually used by the failing connection.`,
 'DNS A    → IPv4 candidate\nDNS AAAA → IPv6 candidate\nIPv6 default router ← Router Advertisement',[['Address candidate','An answer to try, not proof of reachability.'],['Two stacks','Can have different routing and filtering failures.']]),
 S('See why “block all ICMPv6” breaks useful work',`A firewall rule discarding every ICMPv6 message can prevent more than ping. Neighbor discovery can fail, and Packet Too Big feedback can be lost when a path cannot carry a larger packet. Small or already-established exchanges can make the failure appear inconsistent.

Separate necessary control roles from optional echo behavior when designing policy. IPv6’s larger address space also does not authenticate a peer or automatically supply a safe firewall configuration. The same discipline applies as in IPv4: identify the intended destination, selected route, local neighbor, required feedback and allowed application. The new address notation does not eliminate those dependencies.`,
 'Blanket ICMPv6 drop\n→ Neighbor Discovery may fail\n→ Packet Too Big feedback may fail\n→ Ordinary application connectivity can break',[['Neighbor resolution','A prerequisite for local delivery.'],['Size feedback','Helps senders adapt packets to a path limit.']])
 ],[
 Q('Explain','This laptop sends IPv6 successfully without ARP. How did it obtain local neighbor information?','Which messages replace ARP’s local-resolution role in this example?', 'Neighbor Discovery uses ICMPv6 Neighbor Solicitation and Advertisement to obtain or verify local link information. Relevant requests use multicast. IPv6 preserves the need for a local next hop while using a different mechanism from ARP.',['ICMPv6 Neighbor Discovery','Solicitation and Advertisement','Multicast rather than IPv4 broadcast']),
 Q('Diagnose','Why can the blanket ICMPv6 rule prevent normal browsing rather than merely hide ping?','Identify two useful functions broken by dropping every ICMPv6 message.', 'It can break Neighbor Discovery needed for local communication and Packet Too Big feedback used to handle path-size constraints. Required control traffic must be considered by message role. Blocking one diagnostic use does not justify treating the whole protocol as optional.',['Neighbor Discovery','Packet-size feedback','Role-specific policy']),
 Q('Distinguish','Which part of the advertisement provides default-router information, and what can DHCPv6 do instead?','Explain how the advertised prefix, router lifetime and DHCPv6 have separate jobs.', 'The advertisement’s router information and lifetime support default-router discovery. Prefix flags can support SLAAC and on-link behavior. DHCPv6 can provide addresses or other options but normally does not supply the IPv4-style default-gateway option.',['Default router from advertisements','Prefix supports address/local behavior','DHCPv6 supplies different configuration'])
 ],['Read router lifetime and prefix flags.','Form and check an address under the configured method.','Use ICMPv6 neighbor information for local forwarding.','Test the actual IPv4 or IPv6 path and preserve required control feedback.'], 'A missing ARP exchange is expected in IPv6. Also, obtaining an AAAA answer is not a reachability test.'),
 'tcp-connect':C('A client opens port 443 with sequence number 1000. Read the three replies precisely, then decide what success has—and has not—proved.',[
 S('Establish the two endpoints and their sequence state',`The client uses temporary port 53000 to contact a service listening on 443. The IP addresses and ports distinguish the two endpoints of this TCP connection. The opening SYN announces initial sequence number 1000; the server’s SYN announces 7000.

The acknowledgement 1001 says the peer expects the position after the client’s SYN. SYN consumes one position in TCP sequence space even without an HTTP byte. The client’s acknowledgement 7001 does the corresponding job for the server’s SYN. This exchange is the conventional three-way opening. Reading the numbers makes “handshake” a concrete synchronization of state rather than a ceremonial name for connecting.`,
 'Client :53000 → server :443  SYN seq=1000\nServer → client             SYN,ACK seq=7000 ack=1001\nClient → server             ACK seq=1001 ack=7001',[['1001','Acknowledges the SYN at sequence 1000.'],['7001','Acknowledges the server SYN at 7000.']]),
 S('Keep separate connections separate',`Another browser connection can use source port 53001 to the same server port. The server can listen on 443 while maintaining many distinct established connections. The listening service’s port is not a unique identifier for the entire conversation.

Applications access communication endpoints through operating-system APIs commonly called sockets. A socket API call accepting a connection does not establish a logged-in web session, and a destination port number does not authenticate what is listening there. Endpoint identifiers organize delivery; the later application and security exchanges establish other kinds of state. The distinction matters when a scanner or log reports that “443 is open”: that is narrower than a successful authenticated web transaction.`,
 'Connection A: client:53000 ↔ server:443\nConnection B: client:53001 ↔ server:443\nOne listening port, two distinct connections',[['Source port','Helps distinguish simultaneous client connections.'],['Port convention','Does not prove a valid or trustworthy HTTPS service.']]),
 S('Interpret refusal differently from silence',`If a connection attempt receives a TCP reset, an endpoint or intermediary actively rejected it under those conditions. If SYNs receive no usable reply, filtering, loss, routing problems or an unresponsive endpoint remain possible explanations. Silence alone does not identify which one occurred.

An established connection can also end in different ways. FIN normally participates in an orderly close of one direction; RST aborts a connection. Do not translate every reset into “the server crashed,” or every retry into “the firewall blocked it.” The flags and timing narrow the question. Endpoint logs and another observation point can distinguish remaining possibilities.`,
 'Attempt A: SYN → RST reply\nAttempt B: SYN → (no reply), retry → (no reply)\nEstablished connection: FIN and RST have different roles',[['RST response','Active rejection/abort evidence.'],['No response','Several path or endpoint explanations remain.']]),
 S('Explain a certificate failure after this successful opening',`After the three packets, the client sends TLS handshake data. Suppose the browser then reports a certificate hostname mismatch. The TCP opening worked with a responder. The error is now about matching the intended web identity, not a failure to exchange the opening TCP packets.

Inspect the exact hostname, certificate names and reached endpoint, including any proxy or virtual-host selection. Do not suppress identity validation merely because a port accepted a connection. TCP gives a byte-stream transport; it does not certify the business, sign in the user or guarantee that the next application message succeeds. A useful troubleshooting report states which stage succeeded before the observed error.`,
 'TCP: SYN → SYN,ACK → ACK completed\nTLS: certificate name does not match URL\nHTTP: no successful response established by this evidence',[['Confirmed','A transport connection to a responding endpoint.'],['Still failing','TLS identity validation in the stated observation.']])
 ],[
 Q('Trace','Explain all three opening messages and both acknowledgement values in this trace.','What does each side learn during SYN 1000, SYN-ACK 7000/1001, ACK 7001?', 'Both sides establish initial sequence state and acknowledge the other side’s SYN. The server uses 1001 because SYN 1000 consumes one position; the client uses 7001 for the same reason. The exchange supplies evidence of communication in both directions, not application authentication.',['Both initial sequence numbers','Each SYN acknowledged','Shared transport state']),
 Q('Diagnose','The displayed opening completes, then TLS reports a hostname mismatch. Which stage worked and what would you inspect?','Why does an accepted connection on 443 not resolve the later certificate error?', 'TCP connectivity to the responder worked. Compare the URL’s expected name with the presented certificate, reached endpoint and proxy/hosting selection. TLS validation is separate from accepting TCP, and HTTP or application success has not yet been established.',['TCP stage succeeded','Expected versus certified name','TLS and application remain separate']),
 Q('Calculate','What acknowledgement covers SYN seq=1000, and why is it not 1000?','Explain ack=1001 without inventing an HTTP byte in the SYN.', 'The value is 1001 because SYN consumes one sequence position. An acknowledgement identifies the next expected sequence number. That arithmetic describes TCP control state here; it is not evidence of one byte of application data.',['1001','SYN consumes sequence space','Next expected position'])
 ],['Identify both addresses and ports.','Read each SYN and corresponding acknowledgement.','Distinguish rejection from missing replies.','Inspect TLS or HTTP separately after transport establishment.'], 'A completed TCP opening is not a completed HTTPS transaction, even when the destination port is 443.'),
 'tcp-stream':C('A 300-byte application message arrives with its middle 100 bytes missing. Work out what the receiver can acknowledge and when the application can continue.',[
 S('Locate a gap with byte positions',`Imagine this connection is already established and these numbers describe application bytes. The receiver gets 1000–1099, misses 1100–1199, then receives 1200–1299. Its contiguous prefix stops before byte 1100. A cumulative acknowledgement therefore remains 1100.

The later range can be stored out of order, and selective acknowledgements can report that it arrived. But the ordinary ordered byte stream delivered to the application cannot advance past the missing range. TCP is tracking positions, not labeling each packet as a complete business message. Sequence numbers make the loss and the stalled delivery precise enough for the sender and receiver to recover.`,
 'Received: 1000–1099 → ACK 1100\nMissing:  1100–1199\nReceived: 1200–1299 → cumulative ACK 1100\nAfter gap recovered → ACK can advance to 1300',[['ACK 1100','Next contiguous byte expected.'],['Later bytes','Can be known to TCP without passing the delivery gap.']]),
 S('Reconstruct messages despite arbitrary read boundaries',`Suppose the sender writes the text HELLO and WORLD in two calls. TCP can deliver the ten bytes through one read, two reads or several reads. A receiver that assumes “one read equals one message” can misparse correct traffic.

The application needs framing: perhaps a length prefix, a delimiter with escaping, or an established protocol’s message structure. The examples below all preserve the same ordered bytes despite different chunk boundaries. TCP’s retransmission and ordering repair transport delivery, but they do not choose where HELLO ends as an application concept. That belongs to the protocol using the stream. This distinction is useful whenever you write socket code, parsers or proxies.`,
 'Writes: "HELLO" then "WORLD"\nValid reads: ["HELLOWORLD"]\nAlso valid: ["HE", "LLOW", "ORLD"]\nApplication framing must recover its own messages',[['Byte order','Preserved by the stream.'],['Write boundaries','Not promised as receive-call boundaries.']]),
 S('Separate a full receiver from a crowded network',`Now the receiver advertises 64 KiB of available receive capacity, but the path’s bottleneck is congested. Flow control says how much the receiver can accept. Congestion control adjusts how much the sender should inject into the path. A generous receive window does not remove that path limit.

Conversely, a quiet path does not make a receiver with no buffer space ready for unlimited data. Both mechanisms can constrain a connection for different reasons. When interpreting a throughput problem, inspect which resource is limiting progress instead of treating every pause as retransmission. The exact congestion algorithm can vary; the receiver-versus-path distinction remains useful across algorithms.`,
 'Receiver available window: 64 KiB\nPath: bottleneck queue filling / loss signals\nReceiver capacity ≠ available network capacity',[['Flow control','Protects the receiving endpoint.'],['Congestion control','Responds to conditions along the path.']]),
 S('Do not turn a transport ACK into a business guarantee',`The server’s TCP implementation can acknowledge bytes before the application finishes parsing them, charges a card or writes a durable database record. If the application crashes after receipt, the sender cannot infer a completed transaction from the TCP ACK.

An application response must define what completion means. Even that response may require idempotency or transaction identifiers when retrying after uncertainty. TCP also cannot guarantee eventual success through a permanently failed path; timeouts and errors remain possible. Reliable ordered delivery is a powerful transport contract, but it is bounded. The packet trace should be interpreted at the level where the acknowledgement is defined.`,
 'TCP ACK: received bytes at transport\nApplication reply: meaning defined by the application\nDurable completion: needs the application’s own guarantee',[['Transport receipt','Not a database commit.'],['Persistent failure','Can still produce a timeout or connection error.']])
 ],[
 Q('Predict','Two writes contain HELLO and WORLD. Must the receiver make exactly two reads?','Why can the displayed three-read split still be correct TCP delivery?', 'No. TCP preserves ordered bytes rather than write or packet boundaries. The application can receive combined or split chunks and must reconstruct messages using its own framing. All ten bytes can be correct even when the read-call boundaries differ from the sends.',['Ordered bytes','Read boundaries can differ','Application framing needed']),
 Q('Calculate','With 1100–1199 missing and 1200–1299 received, what is the cumulative ACK and why?','Explain why ACK stays at 1100 despite later received bytes.', 'ACK remains 1100, the next byte expected in the contiguous sequence. Selective acknowledgement can describe later received ranges, but the gap still blocks ordered application delivery. Once the missing 100 bytes arrive, the contiguous acknowledgement can advance through 1299 to 1300.',['ACK 1100','Gap controls cumulative progress','Later ranges may be selectively acknowledged']),
 Q('Distinguish','Why can sending still be limited when the receiver advertises 64 KiB of room?','Which mechanism addresses the path bottleneck rather than the receiver’s buffer?', 'Congestion control can limit sending because the path is overloaded even when flow control permits more bytes. The advertised receive window concerns endpoint capacity. Both restrictions can apply simultaneously, so receiver space alone does not predict usable throughput.',['Flow control: receiver','Congestion control: path','Both can constrain sending'])
 ],['Mark the received byte ranges.','Find the next missing contiguous byte.','Reassemble application messages independently of read boundaries.','Separate receiver capacity, path congestion and application completion.'], 'A TCP ACK reports transport-level receipt. It does not certify a message boundary or a completed business transaction.'),
 udp:C('A position update becomes stale while a newer update arrives. Decide what the application should do instead of assuming every missing datagram needs the same recovery.',[
 S('Put meaning and sequence into each update',`A game client sends positions 100, 101 and 102. Update 101 is lost and 102 arrives first. UDP preserves the boundaries of datagrams that are delivered, but does not supply ordered delivery or resend the missing one for the application.

The application can put its own sequence or timestamp in each update. A newer position may make an older one obsolete; waiting for every old state could harm responsiveness. This is a choice about the meaning of the data, not a universal instruction to ignore loss. Loss, duplication and reordering must be considered in the protocol design even when a quick local test happens to deliver everything in order.`,
 'Sent: #100 at x=10, #101 at x=11, #102 at x=12\nObserved arrival: #102, then #100\n#101 never arrives in this example',[['Sequence field','Application-defined information for recognizing newer state.'],['Datagram contract','Does not itself repair this order or loss.']]),
 S('Choose a different policy for a request that must get an answer',`A name query has different meaning from a disposable position update. The client can assign an identifier, wait for a matching response and retry or fail after a timeout. The protocol above UDP defines how to recognize replies, avoid confusion and handle duplicate work.

A successful local send call says the system accepted the datagram for sending. It does not prove that a remote application processed it. A meaningful response supplies additional evidence, with the guarantee determined by the protocol. Responsible congestion behavior and packet-size limits are also needed; a minimal transport does not remove the application designer’s obligations to the network.`,
 'Request id=73 →\nWait for matching response id=73\nNo usable reply → timeout / retry policy\nSend accepted locally ≠ remote processing completed',[['Identifier','Associates a reply with a request.'],['Timeout policy','Must be supplied above UDP rather than assumed.']]),
 S('See how a richer transport can use UDP as its carrier',`QUIC places its own connection state and reliable stream mechanisms over UDP datagrams. It can retransmit lost information, manage congestion and protect communication while using UDP’s delivery substrate. Thus “UDP itself does not retransmit” and “QUIC recovers lost data” are compatible statements.

The same layering idea appears in application reliability over datagrams. Ask which protocol supplies a guarantee. Describing every UDP-based service as unreliable, stateless or unencrypted erases the work done above the carrier. It also leads to bad diagnosis: seeing UDP in a capture does not tell you whether the user’s data is encrypted or whether the application handles losses.`,
 'HTTP/3 → QUIC reliable streams + security → UDP\nPosition app → chosen freshness policy → UDP\nSame carrier, different higher-level guarantees',[['QUIC','Adds transport behavior above UDP.'],['UDP label','Does not describe every property of its payload protocol.']]),
 S('Respect size and capacity even without a TCP connection',`A datagram large enough to exceed a usable path size may be fragmented or dropped depending on IP version and circumstances. Losing one needed fragment can prevent reassembly of the entire datagram. Smaller safe payloads and path-aware design can avoid depending on fragmentation.

UDP also supplies no built-in congestion control. An application should not respond to a crowded path by transmitting without limits merely because it has no TCP window. “Connectionless” describes UDP’s service model, not permission to ignore shared capacity or assume anonymity. Evaluate freshness, reliability, size, congestion and security as separate design questions.`,
 'Large application datagram\n→ exceeds a path/link constraint\n→ fragmentation or drop may prevent useful delivery\nApplication needs a size and congestion policy',[['Datagram boundary','Does not guarantee one conveniently sized link packet.'],['Capacity','Still shared with other network users.']])
 ],[
 Q('Predict','Updates #102 and #100 arrive in that order while #101 is absent. What outcomes must the app handle?','Why can a position app prefer #102 rather than waiting for every old update?', 'UDP can lose, duplicate or reorder datagrams. An application sequence can identify #102 as newer and let the app discard stale state if its semantics permit. A request needing reliable completion could choose a different timeout/retry policy. The application must define the handling.',['Loss/reordering/duplication','Application freshness information','Recovery policy depends on meaning']),
 Q('Explain','How can QUIC recover missing stream data while using UDP?','Which layer supplies reliability in the HTTP/3 stack shown here?', 'UDP carries datagrams, while QUIC implements connection state, reliable streams, loss recovery, congestion control and integrated security above it. A lower layer’s minimal guarantee does not prevent a higher layer from providing stronger behavior. Attribute each property to the protocol that supplies it.',['UDP as carrier','QUIC reliability','Layer-specific guarantees']),
 Q('Reason','The local send for request 73 succeeded. What additional evidence would establish a remote reply?','Why does sending request 73 not prove the remote application processed it?', 'Local acceptance does not establish delivery or processing. A matching reply with the defined identifier supplies protocol-level evidence of remote handling, according to what that reply means. Timeout, retry and duplicate handling still need explicit rules.',['Local acceptance is limited','Matching reply adds evidence','Reply semantics and retry policy matter'])
 ],['Assign meaning to each datagram and its identifier.','Consider loss, duplication and reordering.','Choose freshness or reliability behavior for the task.','Account for packet size, congestion and protection separately.'], 'UDP is a carrier contract. The application or a higher transport determines whether lost information is retried and whether the content is encrypted.')
 ,dns:C('A lookup for www.docs.example gets one answer now and an older address after a server change. Trace who answered and which cache can explain the difference.',[
 S('Follow referrals instead of imagining one giant address book',`A client asks its configured recursive resolver for an A record. In this fictional namespace, the resolver has no relevant cached answer or referral. It starts with root infrastructure, follows a referral toward the example top-level namespace and then toward authoritative service for docs.example.

The authoritative answer supplies 198.51.100.20. Root and intermediate referrals need not contain that final address; they point toward responsibility for narrower parts of the namespace. The client ordinarily asks the recursive resolver, which does this work on its behalf. The sequence is authored to illustrate delegation, not a claim that the reserved example name resolves on the public Internet.`,
 'Client → recursive resolver: A www.docs.example?\nResolver → root: referral toward top-level service\nResolver → top-level: referral toward docs.example\nResolver → authoritative: A 198.51.100.20, TTL 300',[['Recursive resolver','Obtains answers for the client.'],['Authoritative server','Publishes data for the relevant zone.']]),
 S('Count down the cache lifetime',`At 10:00 the resolver caches the address with a 300-second lifetime. At 10:01 the authoritative server is changed to .21. A lookup through this resolver at 10:02 can still receive the usable .20 cache entry, with about 180 seconds of its original lifetime remaining.

Updating the authoritative data does not erase every downstream copy. Different resolvers can have copies populated at different times, so users can temporarily see different values. Cached referrals can also shorten future resolution without supplying the final answer themselves. DNS TTL is a time-based cache value in seconds; it is unrelated to decrementing an IP packet’s hop limit at routers.`,
 '10:00 cache .20, TTL 300 s\n10:01 authoritative changes to .21\n10:02 cached .20 may remain usable (~180 s left)\nLater lookup after ordinary expiry → refresh required',[['300 seconds','Ordinary permitted cache reuse from receipt, subject to DNS rules.'],['Different caches','Can expire at different times.']]),
 S('Read record types as different kinds of information',`An A record supplies an IPv4 address and an AAAA record an IPv6 address. A CNAME supplies an alias whose target must be resolved appropriately. A name can have several relevant records, while one address can serve many website names.

Traditional DNS uses UDP and TCP on port 53. A truncated UDP answer can lead to a TCP retry; TCP is not reserved solely for zone transfers. A browser may instead use an encrypted resolver transport. The lookup answers a name-data question. It does not create the later web connection, authenticate its certificate or deliver an HTTP file. Keep the resolver path separate from the subsequent path toward the selected web endpoint.`,
 'www.docs.example CNAME edge.docs.example\nedge.docs.example A    198.51.100.20\nedge.docs.example AAAA 2001:db8:42::20\nOne name can lead to multiple address candidates',[['CNAME','An alias, not a MAC address or an HTTP redirect.'],['A / AAAA','IPv4 / IPv6 address records.']]),
 S('Diagnose a stale answer using the right observation points',`Suppose one colleague sees .21 while your browser still connects to .20. Record the exact name and record type, which resolver each client used, and which cached state was available. A browser or operating system can also hold useful name results, so comparing only authoritative data with the final connection can skip an intermediate cache.

A difference does not always imply a fault: split DNS or serving policies can intentionally produce different answers. First establish the intended configuration. Then check the value and remaining lifetime at the resolver that actually served your client. The investigation becomes a chain of observations instead of the vague instruction to “flush DNS” without finding which state mattered.`,
 'Authoritative now: .21\nResolver A: .20 with remaining lifetime\nResolver B: .21\nClient path may also reuse a local cached result',[['Exact resolver','Determines which cache you are inspecting.'],['Exact record type','A and AAAA are separate lookup data.']])
 ],[
 Q('Trace','Who performs the referral chain for the client in this cache-miss example?','Explain root, top-level and authoritative roles for this fictional lookup.', 'The client normally asks its recursive resolver. Without relevant cache information, the resolver follows referrals through the hierarchy until it reaches authoritative data for the relevant zone, then returns and caches the answer as allowed. The root need not contain every final web address.',['Client uses recursive resolver','Delegation narrows responsibility','Authoritative data supplies final record']),
 Q('Calculate','Why can .20 still be returned at 10:02 after the 10:01 authoritative change?','Use TTL 300 at 10:00 to explain two users seeing .20 and .21.', 'The earlier cached copy can ordinarily remain usable until about 10:05. At 10:02 roughly 180 seconds remain. A resolver fetching after the change can hold .21 while another still has .20. Authoritative changes do not instantly invalidate every cache.',['Existing cache lifetime','About 180 seconds remain','Different cache population times']),
 Q('Distinguish','Compare the 300-second DNS TTL with a packet’s IP TTL.','Why would decrementing a router hop count not expire this cached answer?', 'DNS TTL limits ordinary cache reuse over time. IP TTL is decremented in forwarding and limits packet circulation. They apply to different state, with different units and mechanisms. A shared abbreviation does not make them one counter.',['Cache lifetime in seconds','Forwarding hop limit','Different state and roles'])
 ],['Ask the configured recursive resolver.','Follow delegation only when relevant cached information is absent.','Read the record type and cache lifetime.','Inspect the actual resolver and client cache path when answers differ.'], 'A resolver supplies name information, not proof of website health. An authoritative update does not erase every cached copy at once.'),
 'dns-trust':C('Three users report “DNS is broken,” but one gets NXDOMAIN, one gets SERVFAIL and one gets silence. Use the differences, then examine what encrypted DNS actually protects.',[
 S('Treat a received negative answer differently from silence',`The first user received a DNS message saying the queried name does not exist. That is NXDOMAIN, a negative answer. The second received SERVFAIL, meaning the resolver could not complete resolution successfully. The third received no usable reply within the wait period.

These observations deserve different next tests. Check the exact spelling, intended namespace and negative-cache state for NXDOMAIN. Investigate the reason for resolver failure in SERVFAIL, which can include validation or upstream problems. For a timeout, investigate the configured resolver, path, filtering and loss. None by itself proves the web application is down, because the attempted web connection may not yet exist.`,
 'User A: status NXDOMAIN\nUser B: status SERVFAIL\nUser C: no usable response before timeout',[['NXDOMAIN','A received answer about name nonexistence.'],['Timeout','Absence of a usable reply, not an asserted negative answer.']]),
 S('Encrypt the route to the resolver without making it infallible',`Now the client uses DNS over HTTPS, or DoH, to contact a chosen resolver. That protects this client-to-resolver transport using HTTPS. A suitable on-path observer can no longer simply read or alter the query as ordinary plaintext DNS on that span.

The resolver is the endpoint of that protection and still sees the query. Its answer is not guaranteed correct merely because it arrived over an encrypted channel. DNS over TLS, or DoT, similarly protects a resolver transport using TLS. These mechanisms change who can observe or modify a particular hop; they do not make the resolver incapable of deception or make all destination metadata disappear from the wider web connection.`,
 'Client ═ protected DoH/DoT connection ═ resolver\nResolver can read the query it receives\nLater web connection has separate endpoints and protection',[['Transport endpoint','The resolver can access the query after decryption.'],['Scope','Protection applies to this span, not every later network observation.']]),
 S('Authenticate signed data with a different mechanism',`DNSSEC validation can check signed DNS data through a chain of trust. In a signed zone, a substituted address should not pass validation merely because an intermediary supplied it. A validating resolver can reject data whose required authentication fails.

This addresses origin authenticity and integrity of signed DNS data, not concealment of the query. Unsigned zones, validation configuration and failures need careful interpretation. DNSSEC does not certify that the site’s business is honest, and encrypted DNS does not replace data validation. Think of the two questions independently: was this transport protected, and is this signed data authentic under the validation chain? Both can matter in the same lookup.`,
 'Encrypted resolver transport → protection of one connection\nDNSSEC validation → authenticity/integrity of signed DNS data\nNeither claim alone means “the destination is trustworthy”',[['Confidentiality','A transport can hide query content from observers on its span.'],['Signed data','Provides a distinct origin/integrity check.']]),
 S('Choose a comparison that does not erase the cause',`If changing resolvers makes the page load, you have a useful comparison, but not yet a complete explanation. The previous resolver could hold stale negative data, have an upstream failure or be serving an intentional internal namespace. A different network can legitimately produce a different answer.

Record the exact query, response code, record type and resolver before changing settings. Compare relevant cache lifetimes and validation behavior when possible. That record lets you distinguish a configuration mistake from a transient path failure or an intended split-DNS policy. Replacing the resolver and declaring success can otherwise hide a reproducible problem the app will encounter again later.`,
 'Before change: exact name + record type + resolver + result\nAfter change: compare like with like\nDifferent answer may be fault, cache, or intended policy',[['Comparison','Can narrow a hypothesis without proving a unique cause.'],['Split DNS','An internal name may require its intended resolver.']])
 ],[
 Q('Diagnose','What different next tests fit User A’s NXDOMAIN and User C’s timeout?','Why should these two “DNS broken” reports not receive the same explanation?', 'NXDOMAIN is a received negative answer, so inspect the exact name, namespace and relevant negative cache. A timeout lacks a usable reply, so inspect resolver reachability, filtering, availability and loss. Both are narrower than a conclusion about the web application.',['Answer versus no reply','Different hypotheses/tests','Do not infer web-app outage']),
 Q('Security','Can the chosen DoH resolver read a query or supply an unwanted address?','Which trust remains after encrypting the client-to-resolver connection?', 'Yes. The resolver terminates the connection and sees its query. Transport encryption protects that span from relevant on-path interference but does not independently prove the data is correct. Resolver trust and applicable DNSSEC validation are separate concerns.',['Resolver sees content','Protected transport scope','Data authenticity remains separate']),
 Q('Explain','Why might you want both encrypted DNS transport and DNSSEC validation?','Explain the separate questions answered by DoH/DoT and signed DNS data.', 'Encrypted transport protects the connection to the resolver. DNSSEC supports authenticity and integrity of signed zone data. DNSSEC does not conceal queries, while transport encryption alone does not prove data came from the intended authoritative source. They address complementary properties.',['Transport protection','Signed-data authenticity/integrity','Different, complementary roles'])
 ],['Record the exact response or absence of one.','Investigate the relevant resolver and cache.','Separate encrypted transport from signed-data validation.','Use comparisons to distinguish causes rather than merely hide a symptom.'], 'An encrypted channel to a resolver can still deliver a wrong answer. A DNS error code is not a diagnosis of the web server.'),
 'tls-identity':C('You request accounts.example, but the server presents a certificate for files.example. The packets arrived—so why should the browser refuse the connection?',[
 S('Compare the requested identity with the certificate',`The URL names accounts.example. The certificate’s subject alternative names include files.example but do not cover the requested name. A route reaching this endpoint and an accepted TCP connection do not fix that mismatch.

The browser is trying to establish a protected conversation with the intended identity, not just any machine that answered at an address. Many sites can share one address, and misrouting or virtual-host configuration can select the wrong certificate. A useful investigation compares the exact name in the URL with the names actually covered by the certificate, before treating the issue as a generic inability to connect.`,
 'URL: https://accounts.example/\nTCP to selected endpoint: connected\nCertificate DNS name: files.example\nExpected name accounts.example: not covered',[['URL name','Sets the identity the browser intends to authenticate.'],['Certificate names','Must cover that expected name under validation rules.']]),
 S('Validate more than the printed name',`Even a matching name is not the only check. The browser evaluates the certificate’s validity and a suitable chain toward an accepted trust anchor, along with other applicable rules. The peer also proves the relevant private-key capability during the authenticated handshake.

A certificate is signed public information binding claims to a key. Someone copying the public file does not thereby acquire the corresponding private key. Think of the certificate as an identity claim supported by a trust system, and the handshake proof as evidence that the responding peer controls the key capability needed for that claim. Neither a decorative certificate icon nor possession of the public document alone supplies all of this.`,
 'Expected name → matching certificate names\nCertificate → issuer chain → accepted trust anchor\nHandshake → proof using relevant private-key capability',[['Chain','Relates the certificate to the configured trust system.'],['Key possession','Cannot normally be demonstrated by copying only a public certificate.']]),
 S('Explain the misleading “works by IP” experiment',`Replacing accounts.example in the URL with its IP address changes the requested identity. The certificate may cover the hostname but not the literal IP. The change can also affect server-name selection in TLS and the HTTP authority used for hosting.

Therefore, an HTTPS error for the bare IP is not a clean equivalent of the hostname test. You may have confirmed some network reachability while creating a different identity or hosting failure. Keep the intended name intact when testing its TLS behavior, and inspect the reached endpoint separately. A good test changes the variable you intend to investigate instead of silently changing several protocol contexts at once.`,
 'https://accounts.example/ → expect accounts.example\nhttps://198.51.100.20/     → expect this literal IP identity\nSame address path can produce different validation results',[['Expected identity changed','A hostname-only certificate need not cover the IP.'],['Hosting context','Name-dependent service selection can also change.']]),
 S('Know what a valid certificate does not promise',`An attacker can control account-help.example and obtain a valid certificate for that different name. The resulting TLS identity binding can be correct while the site’s content is malicious. Nothing in that observation requires TLS to be broken.

The certificate is not an endorsement of business honesty, a security audit of the application or proof that the user chose the intended domain. Conversely, a copied legitimate certificate without its key does not normally let an attacker impersonate accounts.example. Keep those two cases distinct: authenticating the wrong chosen name and defeating authentication for the correct name are different problems.`,
 'Intended: accounts.example\nMisleading different site: account-help.example\nValid certificate for the second name ≠ honesty endorsement',[['Exact name','The user must still select the intended service identity.'],['Trust model','Certifies specific identity/key claims, not every property of the site.']])
 ],[
 Q('Security','Can copying the accounts.example public certificate normally make an attacker its TLS server?','What is missing when the attacker has only the public certificate file?', 'The attacker still needs the relevant private-key capability for the handshake proof, or another way to subvert validation or trust. Certificates are public signed information. Copying one does not normally supply possession of the secret needed to authenticate that identity.',['Certificate is public','Private-key proof needed','Subverted trust is a separate route']),
 Q('Diagnose','Why can a bare-IP URL produce a certificate failure even when the intended hostname works?','Explain which variables change when replacing accounts.example with its address.', 'The expected identity becomes the literal IP, which the certificate may not cover. TLS and HTTP name-based service selection can also change. A working route is compatible with a failed identity check, so preserve the intended hostname when testing its secure service.',['Expected identity changes','Certificate may not cover IP','Hosting selection may differ']),
 Q('Reason','Why can account-help.example be a phishing site with a valid certificate?','What has TLS established—and not established—for the lookalike name?', 'It can correctly authenticate that distinct domain under the trust model. That says nothing by itself about honest intent or whether the user selected the right domain. A valid binding for an attacker-controlled name is not the same as impersonating the intended accounts.example identity.',['Different valid name','Identity is not honesty','Intended domain still matters'])
 ],['Read the exact URL identity.','Compare covered names, chain and validity.','Verify the peer’s key-possession proof.','Distinguish authenticating the wrong chosen domain from breaking the intended identity.'], 'A TCP connection proves less than TLS identity validation; a valid TLS identity proves less than an honest application.'),
 'tls-handshake':C('Two endpoints agree traffic keys without sending those keys across the wire. Follow a fresh TLS 1.3 exchange, then locate where a CDN can read the content.',[
 S('Read the key shares as contributions, not traffic passwords',`In this simplified fresh TLS 1.3 exchange, the client offers parameters and a key share in ClientHello. The server chooses compatible parameters and contributes a key share. With a usual ephemeral key exchange, each endpoint combines its own secret material with the peer’s public contribution to derive shared secrets.

The traffic keys are derived, not transmitted as an ordinary password in the greeting. Seeing the public shares does not by itself give a passive observer those keys. This explanation describes one common fresh-handshake pattern; resumption and pre-shared-key modes change details. State the pattern you are analyzing rather than assuming every valid TLS trace must have identical messages.`,
 'Client → ClientHello + supported parameters + key share\nServer → ServerHello + selected parameters + key share\nBoth derive secrets locally; traffic keys are not sent as a field',[['Key share','A public contribution to the key-agreement process.'],['Local secret','Needed alongside received information to derive the shared result.']]),
 S('Bind the exchange to an authenticated server',`Key agreement alone could create secrets with the wrong endpoint. The server’s authenticated handshake material typically includes a certificate chain and a proof using its private-key capability. Finished messages verify the handshake’s integrity using derived key material.

Certificate-related authentication and bulk encryption use different roles. Modern TLS does not simply encrypt every HTTP byte with the certificate’s public key. Symmetric traffic keys protect application data after the handshake establishes the session. In TLS 1.3, handshake protection also means a passive trace does not necessarily expose certificates as plaintext after ServerHello. What a capture can show depends on the protocol stage and available decryption information.`,
 'Certificate chain → identity claim under trust rules\nCertificateVerify → proof tied to handshake\nFinished → handshake integrity\nDerived symmetric keys → protected application data',[['Authentication','Binds the session to the expected peer under the trust model.'],['Traffic protection','Uses derived symmetric keys rather than public-key encryption of every byte.']]),
 S('Draw two TLS connections when a proxy terminates',`Suppose the browser’s connection ends at a CDN edge. That endpoint decrypts data to serve or proxy the request. It can then establish a different connection to the origin, possibly using separate keys, validation and even a different application protocol version.

Browser-to-edge TLS does not automatically establish protection from edge to origin. An origin configuration must define that second leg. The edge’s ability to read plaintext is a consequence of being a TLS endpoint, not evidence that encryption failed. This is why locating the endpoints matters more than drawing one padlock above an entire multi-hop architecture. Each protected connection has its own scope.`,
 'Browser ═ TLS session A ═ CDN edge\nCDN edge ═ separate TLS session B ═ origin\nPlaintext is available where the edge processes requests',[['Session A','Protection ends at the edge.'],['Session B','Requires its own secure configuration and validation.']]),
 S('Separate encrypted content from visible metadata',`A network observer can still see addresses, packet timing and sizes around an encrypted connection. In deployments with unencrypted ClientHello SNI, a requested server name can also be exposed. Encrypted client hello and encrypted DNS alter particular name observations, depending on deployment and vantage point.

Do not turn “HTTPS hides the page content on this span” into “nobody can observe anything about the destination.” Also keep resumption and early data separate from this fresh example: early application data has additional replay considerations. A useful security explanation names the observer, the span and the property being protected instead of treating encryption as a universal invisibility claim.`,
 'Often visible: endpoint addresses, timing, sizes\nName visibility: depends on DNS / ClientHello protection\nProtected HTTP content: different observation from metadata',[['Observer location','Determines which span and endpoint information is visible.'],['Early data','Needs additional rules beyond this fresh-handshake model.']])
 ],[
 Q('Explain','Why does this handshake need both certificate-related operations and derived traffic keys?','Why is encrypting all HTTP with the certificate public key an incomplete description?', 'Certificate-related operations authenticate the peer and bind the exchange under the trust model. Key agreement derives shared secret material and symmetric traffic keys protect data. Authentication, key agreement and bulk protection are connected but distinct operations.',['Authentication role','Shared-secret derivation','Symmetric traffic protection']),
 Q('Security','Who can read plaintext when session A terminates at the CDN, and what protects the origin leg?','Why does the browser’s padlock not describe a single end-to-end browser-to-origin TLS session here?', 'The terminating edge can process plaintext. The edge-to-origin leg is a separate connection needing its own encryption and validation. Browser-to-edge protection does not prove that second leg is secure; the two sessions have different endpoints and keys.',['Edge sees plaintext','Separate origin session','Separate configuration needed']),
 Q('Reason','What could an observer still learn around this HTTPS connection?','Explain why encrypted HTTP content does not imply that addresses or timing are hidden.', 'Endpoint addresses, timing and sizes can remain visible. Name visibility depends on DNS and ClientHello mechanisms and the observer’s position. Identify the protected content span separately from metadata rather than promising universal concealment.',['Addresses/timing/sizes','Name visibility depends on deployment','Scope and vantage point matter'])
 ],['Identify the handshake pattern and exchanged contributions.','Verify authentication and integrity, not just shared secrets.','Use derived keys for protected traffic.','Draw each terminating TLS connection and its remaining metadata separately.'], 'A TLS terminator can read the content it terminates. Encrypting one connection does not automatically protect a distinct onward connection.'),
 http:C('The browser already has a 12 KiB document tagged v7. Read a conditional request and explain how a network reply can avoid downloading that body again.',[
 S('Translate one request into an application action',`The client wants /notes from docs.example. GET requests a representation of that resource. Host identifies the intended website in this HTTP/1.1 example. If-None-Match supplies the validator of a stored representation, asking the service to apply conditional-request semantics rather than blindly transfer another full copy.

The validator is opaque: the client need not decode v7 into a version number or assume every server constructs tags the same way. This readable message is an authored HTTP/1.1 representation. HTTP/2 and HTTP/3 preserve the request semantics while changing wire framing. Start with what the application is asking before treating the headers as unexplained names.`,
 'GET /notes HTTP/1.1\nHost: docs.example\nIf-None-Match: "v7"\n\nStored body: 12 KiB; stored ETag: "v7"',[['/notes','The requested resource on this authority.'],['If-None-Match','A condition using a stored validator.']]),
 S('Interpret 304 as permission to reuse suitable stored content',`If the applicable condition indicates the stored representation has not changed, the response can be 304 Not Modified. It carries response metadata under the rules but no new full representation body. The client reuses its suitable stored representation and applies relevant updated metadata.

The network exchange still happened. Avoiding a 12 KiB body is different from satisfying the request entirely from a fresh local cache without contacting a server. If the representation changed, the service can return a normal response with the new body and validator instead. The status must be interpreted together with the request, stored state and cache rules; it is not a universal instruction that every client has the content it needs.`,
 'Response: HTTP/1.1 304 Not Modified\nETag: "v7"\nNo new 12 KiB representation body\nClient reuses its applicable stored copy',[['304','Conditional-response semantics, not an empty replacement page.'],['Network versus transfer','A revalidation can save body bytes while still contacting a responder.']]),
 S('Use status codes to locate the responding layer',`A 404 response says the responding HTTP service did not find a current representation for that target or will not disclose one. A 500 reports a server-side error at that service. Those are real application responses, not the same observation as a DNS timeout or an unanswered SYN.

On an HTTPS exchange, obtaining the response means the relevant protected connection worked sufficiently to carry it. A proxy can produce an error before the intended origin application is reached. Therefore the safest conclusion is that some endpoint answered HTTP. Inspect response context and upstream evidence before asserting that every backend is healthy or that the origin definitely processed the request.`,
 'DNS timeout: no usable lookup reply\nTCP timeout: no established transport from that attempt\nHTTP 500: an endpoint returned an application-protocol response',[['Positive evidence','An HTTP response confirms a responder was reached.'],['Origin uncertainty','An intermediary may have produced the response.']]),
 S('Explain shared addresses and different resources',`Two requests to the same address can name docs.example and shop.example. Host or equivalent authority information lets a server or proxy select the intended website. The method and path then specify the resource action. TLS name selection and certificate validation add related identity context for HTTPS.

Caching rules also depend on more than the URL alone: authorization, freshness and response variation can matter. The example’s reusable document is not permission to cache every user’s private response in a shared store. Keep authority, method, target, validators and policy connected to their purpose. They describe what the application means after the lower layers have supplied a channel.`,
 'Same IP endpoint\nHost: docs.example → documentation service\nHost: shop.example → shop service\nEach then interprets method, path and applicable cache policy',[['Authority','Selects the site context beyond the destination IP.'],['Cache eligibility','Must follow the response and request rules, especially for personal data.']])
 ],[
 Q('Trace','What does the client do after this conditional GET receives 304?','Explain what was saved and what still crossed the network for the v7 document.', 'It reuses its applicable stored representation under caching rules and updated metadata. The conditional exchange occurred, but the server did not send a new full 12 KiB body. This differs from a purely local cache hit with no revalidation request.',['Reuse stored representation','Validator condition','Network exchange still occurred']),
 Q('Diagnose','Why does a genuine HTTP 500 narrow the investigation differently from a DNS timeout?','What did the path establish before the responding endpoint returned this HTTP error?', 'An endpoint was reached sufficiently to return HTTP; for HTTPS its secure exchange worked sufficiently too. The error may come from a proxy, so origin processing remains unproven. A DNS timeout provides no such application-response evidence.',['HTTP responder reached','Earlier path to responder worked','Intermediary may be the source']),
 Q('Explain','How can docs.example and shop.example share an IP but answer differently?','Which request information selects the intended website beyond its network address?', 'Host in HTTP/1.1 or corresponding authority information in later versions identifies the site context. The server or proxy uses it for hosting selection; HTTPS additionally involves TLS naming and validation. The address alone does not identify the full intended web resource.',['Authority/Host','Virtual hosting','Related TLS name context'])
 ],['Read method, authority, target and validator as meaningful request fields.','Compare the condition with stored representation state.','Interpret 304 as reuse under rules, not an empty new document.','Use status and responder identity to distinguish application errors from missing lower-layer exchanges.'], 'A conditional 304 still involves communication. An HTTP error can be produced by an intermediary and does not by itself identify the failing origin component.')
 ,'http-versions':C('Three resources share one connection. A lost byte range delays two resources that already arrived—then QUIC changes which ordering rule applies.',[
 S('Multiplex three resources instead of serializing complete responses',`The browser requests a document, a stylesheet and an image. HTTP/2 can assign their exchanges separate streams and interleave frames over one connection. The image need not finish before the stylesheet starts making application-level progress.

Stream identifiers tell HTTP/2 which logical exchange each frame belongs to. The shared connection can still carry the familiar HTTP methods, status meanings and fields, even though the wire representation differs from the readable HTTP/1.1 text. Multiplexing solves a particular scheduling/framing problem. It does not make the network path lossless or turn every resource into a completely independent physical channel.`,
 'HTTP/2 connection over TCP\nStream 1: document\nStream 3: stylesheet\nStream 5: image\nFrames from several streams may be interleaved',[['Stream identifier','Associates frames with a logical exchange.'],['One TCP connection','Still underlies all of these HTTP/2 streams.']]),
 S('See the shared TCP gap stop unrelated HTTP frames',`Suppose the TCP byte range carrying part of the image is missing, while later bytes carrying stylesheet frames arrive. TCP presents one ordered stream to its application. It cannot deliver those later bytes past the gap just because HTTP/2 would consider the stylesheet a separate stream.

This is a cross-stream delivery delay caused by the shared TCP ordering layer. The lost range is identified in TCP sequence space, not by an image-specific delivery contract. HTTP multiplexing does not override the lower transport’s rule. A trace can therefore show that later packets arrived at the receiver while an application still waits to receive usable data beyond the gap.`,
 'TCP bytes 1000–1099: received\nTCP bytes 1100–1199: missing (image-related frames)\nTCP bytes 1200–1299: received (stylesheet frames)\nTCP cannot deliver later bytes past the gap',[['TCP sequence space','Shared across the connection, not per HTTP/2 stream.'],['Arrival versus delivery','Packets can arrive before ordered bytes become available to HTTP.']]),
 S('Move reliable stream delivery into QUIC',`HTTP/3 maps HTTP exchanges onto QUIC streams. QUIC is carried in UDP datagrams and supplies reliable delivery, loss recovery, congestion control and integrated secure connection behavior. It can order data per stream rather than expose all exchanges through one TCP sequence space.

Missing image-stream data therefore need not prevent delivery of already-received stylesheet-stream data solely because of TCP-wide ordering. This is the specific blockage the example changes. It does not imply UDP itself added reliability or that no interaction remains. QUIC provides the richer transport behavior above its datagram carrier, and application/protocol dependencies can still affect progress.`,
 'HTTP/3 → QUIC stream A: image gap\n       → QUIC stream B: received stylesheet data\n       → UDP carrier\nStream B need not wait solely for A’s missing range',[['Per-stream ordering','Separates this delivery constraint.'],['QUIC','Provides reliability and congestion control above UDP.']]),
 S('Predict what blocking UDP does—and does not—show',`A network that prevents usable UDP can prevent QUIC on that path. A browser and server may still support HTTPS using HTTP/2 or HTTP/1.1 over TCP. Whether fallback occurs depends on implementation, configuration and available endpoints.

Streams also share bandwidth and congestion effects. A crowded path, slow server or application dependency can delay several resources even when per-stream ordering works as designed. HTTP/3 is not a blanket guarantee of lower latency in every deployment. Diagnose the negotiated protocol and the limiting resource rather than equating a version label with performance. The practical distinction is which layers provide ordering and which work remains shared.`,
 'UDP path unavailable → HTTP/3 may fail\nTCP path usable + support → HTTP/2 or HTTP/1.1 may work\nIndependent stream ordering ≠ independent bandwidth',[['Fallback','A supported alternative, not proof every site must load.'],['Shared capacity','Can still couple otherwise separate streams.']])
 ],[
 Q('Reason','Why can the missing image-related TCP range delay received stylesheet data in HTTP/2?','Which ordering rule explains this delay despite separate HTTP streams?', 'HTTP/2 frames share a single ordered TCP byte stream. A gap holds up later byte delivery regardless of which logical HTTP stream those later frames belong to. Application-level multiplexing does not remove that lower-layer constraint.',['Shared TCP byte stream','Gap blocks later delivery','Separate HTTP streams do not override TCP']),
 Q('Explain','What changes when these exchanges use HTTP/3 over QUIC?','Why can received stream B data progress despite missing stream A data?', 'QUIC supplies reliable per-stream ordering above UDP. A missing range in one stream need not block received data on another solely because of TCP-wide sequence ordering. Shared congestion, capacity and application dependencies can still couple performance.',['QUIC over UDP','Per-stream ordering','Shared limits remain']),
 Q('Predict','The network blocks UDP but permits TCP 443. Must every HTTPS request fail?','How could this browser still load the site without a working QUIC path?', 'Not necessarily. If both sides and the deployment support it, TCP-based HTTP/2 or HTTP/1.1 can provide HTTPS. Blocking QUIC’s carrier does not establish failure of every possible secure web transport. Confirm the actual negotiated alternative and its path.',['QUIC path affected','TCP alternatives may exist','Fallback depends on support/configuration'])
 ],['Identify the logical HTTP streams.','Locate any shared TCP byte gap.','Compare QUIC’s per-stream delivery rule.','Account for fallback, shared capacity and application dependencies.'], 'Reducing one kind of head-of-line blocking does not eliminate loss, congestion or every cross-stream dependency.'),
 sessions:C('A browser returns to /account without logging in again. Trace the session cookie, then explain what a stolen token and a CORS error actually mean.',[
 S('Connect the second request to the first login',`After the application accepts a login, it sets an opaque session identifier. The browser stores the cookie if it meets the relevant rules and later attaches it to eligible requests. The server maps the identifier to session state and checks whether the requested action is allowed.

The cookie below is invented, not a real credential. Its value need not encode a readable username; it can simply be a lookup handle. A TCP connection or TLS handshake alone does not establish the logged-in application identity. Several requests and even new transport connections can use the same valid session token, subject to expiry, revocation and application policy.`,
 'Response: Set-Cookie: sid=demo-session; Secure; HttpOnly; SameSite=Lax; Path=/\nLater request to the same service:\nGET /account\nCookie: sid=demo-session',[['sid','Application-recognized session identifier.'],['Later Cookie field','Returns eligible stored state; it is not a fresh password login.']]),
 S('Assign each cookie attribute its actual job',`Secure restricts cookie transmission to secure contexts under cookie rules. HttpOnly blocks common JavaScript cookie APIs from exposing the cookie value. The browser can still send an HttpOnly cookie automatically on an eligible request. SameSite controls some cross-site sending behavior.

These controls are useful for different reasons. Script injection can still cause actions through the victim’s browser even when the script cannot directly read an HttpOnly value. Domain and Path also shape cookie scope but should not be treated as strong isolation guarantees on their own. Read the attributes as targeted constraints rather than three interchangeable words meaning that the entire session is safe.`,
 'Secure   → transmission restriction\nHttpOnly → script access to cookie value restricted\nSameSite → certain cross-site sending rules\nBrowser can still attach an eligible HttpOnly cookie',[['Value access','Different from automatic request attachment.'],['Cross-site','Not identical to every cross-origin situation.']]),
 S('Explain why stealing the token can bypass a new password prompt',`If sid acts as a bearer credential, another caller able to present it may act as that session holder unless expiry, revocation or additional checks prevent use. Sending the stolen value through a separately valid HTTPS connection does not contradict TLS protection of the victim’s original connection.

TLS protects a span in transit. Tokens can leak through application bugs, logging, device compromise or a compromised endpoint outside that protection’s guarantees. Session storage, rotation, revocation and permission checks matter alongside transport encryption. The right question is which boundary exposed the bearer capability, not whether a padlock should have made every token theft impossible.`,
 'Victim’s TLS connection: protected in transit\nToken exposed elsewhere: application/device/logging boundary\nReplay with valid token: application may recognize the session',[['Bearer credential','Possession can grant use under application rules.'],['Session lifecycle','Limits or revokes that capability.']]),
 S('Use a real browser-policy distinction to interpret a CORS error',`An origin is scheme, host and port. A page at https://app.shop.example and an API at https://api.shop.example are different origins. Suppose the API receives a simple eligible cross-origin request and returns 200, but omits the required CORS response permission. Browser script can be denied access to the response even though the request reached the API.

Other request types require a successful preflight before the substantive request is sent. Credentials add further rules, including explicit response permissions when applicable. CORS is not a replacement for server authentication or authorization, and a CORS error is not universal proof that the server saw nothing. Also keep the site concept used by SameSite distinct from origin.`,
 'Page origin: https://app.shop.example\nAPI origin:  https://api.shop.example\nAPI log: request received, response 200\nBrowser script: response access denied by CORS rules',[['Sending versus reading','They can have different outcomes.'],['Preflight','Some requests are gated before the substantive request.']])
 ],[
 Q('Security','Why can replaying a leaked sid matter even when the original site uses HTTPS correctly?','Which guarantee does TLS leave unresolved about this bearer session token?', 'A valid bearer value can be used by another caller if application checks permit it. TLS protects its connection, not every log, device or storage boundary. Investigate how the capability leaked and how expiry, revocation and permissions limit it.',['Bearer possession may authorize','TLS has a defined span','Application/session controls matter']),
 Q('Distinguish','Explain the Secure, HttpOnly and SameSite attributes in this specific cookie.','Why does HttpOnly not mean that the browser stops sending sid?', 'Secure restricts transmission; HttpOnly limits script access to the value; SameSite affects certain cross-site sends. An eligible HttpOnly cookie can still be attached automatically. These are targeted constraints, not equivalent complete-session defenses.',['Transport restriction','Script value access','Cross-site sending rule']),
 Q('Reason','The API log records a 200 but the page reports CORS failure. How can both be true?','Why must this API still enforce authentication and authorization independently of CORS?', 'A request can reach the API while browser script is barred from reading its response. Some other requests need preflight first. CORS governs browser access behavior and does not enforce the server’s own identity or action permissions for every caller.',['Request arrival versus response access','Preflight affects some requests','Server authorization remains required'])
 ],['Follow Set-Cookie into a later eligible request.','Separate each attribute’s purpose.','Treat bearer-token possession as an application capability.','Interpret CORS using request type, response access and server-side policy.'], 'A CORS error does not always mean no request was sent. HttpOnly protects value access, not every possible action through the browser.'),
 edge:C('The browser loads a page, yet the origin has no matching request log. Follow the CDN cache hit before blaming missing telemetry.',[
 S('Let the edge answer without asking the origin',`The browser reaches a public-facing CDN edge. In this authored case, the edge has a fresh, eligible cached copy of /guide and serves it directly. The origin sees no new request for that operation because none was needed.

This explains why successful user-facing loads do not always have one-to-one origin log entries. A CDN is distributed serving infrastructure, often combining proxying and caching. A reverse proxy accepts client work and forwards selected requests upstream. A load balancer distributes work among backends. One product can perform several of these jobs, so trace the selected action rather than assuming every named component always opens another connection.`,
 'Browser → edge: GET /guide\nEdge cache: eligible fresh HIT\nEdge → browser: 200 + stored representation\nOrigin request for this operation: none',[['Cache hit','Can satisfy the browser without an origin trip.'],['Missing origin log','Expected for this stated path, not proof the load was imaginary.']]),
 S('Draw the separate connections for a miss',`On an applicable cache miss, the edge may contact the origin pool. Browser TLS ends at the edge, and the edge can create a separate origin connection with different endpoint addresses, keys and protocol settings. The origin commonly observes the proxy’s source address at the network layer.

A forwarding header may convey original-client context, but that metadata needs a trusted interpretation. The browser-to-edge connection is not the same transport flow as edge-to-origin. When correlating captures or logs, label each leg and use suitable request identifiers or timing context. Expecting one client source IP and one TCP sequence space to appear unchanged at both points can produce a false diagnosis.`,
 'Browser ═ connection A ═ edge\n                         └─ connection B → origin pool\nOrigin network peer: often the edge/proxy address',[['Connection boundary','Addresses and transport state can differ across the proxy.'],['Correlation','Needs context appropriate to separate exchanges.']]),
 S('Place a gateway error at the leg that failed',`Suppose the browser receives 502 from the edge while the edge’s origin connection is refused or unusable. The client-to-edge application path worked sufficiently for an HTTP response. The origin application did not necessarily receive the request or produce that error body.

Inspect edge upstream logs, origin reachability, TLS validation, timeouts and backend behavior. A health-check label should also be read narrowly: a basic check can pass while a particular request path fails. Conversely, a cache hit may keep some content available during an origin outage. The observed status and serving path tell you which component answered, not automatically which internal subsystem caused the underlying problem.`,
 'Browser ← HTTP 502 from edge\nEdge upstream attempt → unusable response/connection\nQuestion: which leg and backend condition failed?',[['Positive evidence','The edge could answer the client.'],['Unproven origin processing','A proxy can generate the error before origin application handling.']]),
 S('Trust cache and forwarding metadata deliberately',`If arbitrary callers can supply X-Forwarded-For, the text can be forged. An origin should accept and interpret it according to a configured trusted proxy chain, including how proxies sanitize or append values. A header is not self-authenticating proof of the real network source.

Likewise, a shared cache must follow eligibility and variation rules for personalized content. A response for one logged-in user must not become another user’s private page merely because the URL matches. These are concrete trust boundaries inside the serving path: which identity metadata is accepted, and which response can be reused for which request. Proxying does not remove the application’s responsibility for those decisions.`,
 'Untrusted caller: X-Forwarded-For: forged-value\nTrusted proxy policy: sanitize/interpret known chain\nPrivate response: shared reuse requires correct rules',[['Header trust','Depends on the path and trusted emitters.'],['Cache scope','Personalization changes safe reuse conditions.']])
 ],[
 Q('Trace','Why can the browser’s successful /guide load have no matching origin request?','Which connection is unnecessary for this fresh edge cache hit?', 'The edge can serve its eligible cached representation directly. The browser-edge exchange occurs, while an origin request for that operation need not occur. Logs at different observation points therefore need not have identical counts.',['Edge served cache hit','No origin leg needed','Different log vantage points']),
 Q('Diagnose','What does the edge’s 502 prove, and which upstream evidence would you seek?','Why must you not assume the origin application generated this error body?', 'The edge answered the client with HTTP. Its failure report concerns obtaining a suitable upstream result, which might fail before origin application processing. Inspect the origin leg’s connection, TLS, timeout and backend evidence rather than inferring a healthy origin exchange.',['Edge answered','Upstream leg needs investigation','Origin handling not guaranteed']),
 Q('Security','Why is accepting every X-Forwarded-For value as the user’s real IP unsafe?','What makes forwarded client metadata meaningful rather than arbitrary text?', 'A direct or untrusted caller can forge the field. Its interpretation requires a configured trusted proxy path and rules for sanitation or appending. The header alone does not prove the underlying source identity or address.',['Caller can forge text','Trusted proxy-chain handling','Metadata is not self-authenticating'])
 ],['Identify the endpoint that answered the browser.','Check whether a cache hit bypassed the origin leg.','Correlate separate proxy connections correctly.','Validate client metadata and personalized-cache reuse at their trust boundaries.'], 'An origin log is evidence from one serving point. A cache hit or proxy-generated error can produce a browser response without the assumed origin request.'),
 firewalls:C('Replies to outgoing HTTPS are allowed while unsolicited connections are blocked. Then a split VPN carries an internal API but leaves public browsing direct.',[
 S('Read the reply as part of an allowed flow',`The host allows an outgoing TCP connection from local port 53000 to server port 443. Its stateful firewall tracks that flow. A suitable returning packet can be recognized as related to the allowed connection, while a new unsolicited inbound attempt to port 22 can be rejected.

“Inbound traffic is blocked” is therefore too coarse a description. The direction, tracked state and configured rule all matter. A stateful firewall does not merely allow any packet whose source port happens to be 443. Read the actual flow association and policy rather than replacing them with a port-number shortcut. The example describes intended filtering behavior, not a claim about every implementation’s exact tracking rules.`,
 'Allow new outbound: laptop:53000 → server:443\nTrack connection state\nAllow matching reply: server:443 → laptop:53000\nReject unsolicited new inbound: remote → laptop:22',[['State association','Distinguishes replies from unrelated new attempts.'],['Port 443','A convention and policy input, not a blanket safety label.']]),
 S('Place the rule where it can see and protect the right thing',`A host firewall sees traffic at the endpoint. A perimeter firewall sees a different interface and aggregate path. A rule allowing encrypted traffic on 443 does not prove that its application content is benign. A TLS-terminating proxy has a different view from a forwarding device that sees protected payloads.

When a connection fails, identify the enforcing component, matched rule and observation point. “There is a firewall” is not a complete explanation of a drop. The return path can cross a different policy boundary from the forward path. Network translation, flow tracking and filtering may share one box but should still be reasoned about as distinct functions.`,
 'Host rule: endpoint context\nPerimeter rule: network-boundary context\nTLS terminator: can inspect its decrypted content\nForwarder: cannot assume plaintext access',[['Placement','Determines available context and affected traffic.'],['Allowed path','Does not certify every application action as safe.']]),
 S('Use the routing table to explain the split tunnel',`The VPN routes 10.20.0.0/16 through its tunnel interface, while the ordinary default route still uses the home gateway. A request to internal API 10.20.4.8 enters the tunnel. A public web destination can follow the normal direct route.

This is split tunneling. The VPN connection icon reports a tunnel arrangement, not necessarily that every destination uses it. A full-tunnel policy would route a broader set of traffic through the VPN, with deployment-specific exceptions. DNS is also configuration-dependent: a private name may need a resolver reachable through the tunnel even while public traffic remains direct. Inspect routes and resolver behavior rather than inferring the path from the icon.`,
 '10.20.0.0/16 → VPN interface\ndefault      → home gateway\n10.20.4.8    → tunnel\nPublic destination → ordinary route, absent a specific exception',[['Selected prefix','Only matching destinations use this tunnel route.'],['DNS handling','Needs its own suitable resolver/path configuration.']]),
 S('Locate the end of VPN protection',`In a typical remote-access VPN, the protected tunnel ends at the VPN gateway. The gateway then forwards along another path. HTTPS can independently protect the application conversation from the browser to its TLS endpoint.

The tunnel and HTTPS therefore protect different spans and trust different endpoints. A local observer may see the tunnel endpoint while the gateway sees onward destinations according to the arrangement. A VPN does not automatically make the user anonymous, authenticate every web application or repair a compromised device. Draw the two sets of endpoints before describing what is concealed or trusted. “Encrypted somewhere” is not an adequate description of an entire route.`,
 'Laptop ═ VPN tunnel ═ VPN gateway → onward network\nBrowser ═ separate HTTPS protection ═ TLS endpoint\nDifferent endpoints define different protection spans',[['VPN endpoint','The tunnel’s protection terminates here.'],['HTTPS','Can continue protecting application content beyond that gateway.']])
 ],[
 Q('Explain','Why can the firewall allow the reply to port 53000 while rejecting a new inbound attempt to 22?','Which tracked information distinguishes these two inbound observations?', 'The reply can match state for an allowed outgoing flow. The new inbound attempt lacks that association and is evaluated by the policy for new traffic. Actual behavior depends on rules and tracking, not merely the direction label or a familiar source port.',['Flow state','Reply association','Separate new-traffic policy']),
 Q('Security','Why does the VPN not replace HTTPS between this browser and the web service?','What happens to the tunnel’s protection after its gateway?', 'The VPN span ends at the gateway. Onward traffic has its own path and risks; HTTPS can independently protect content to its TLS endpoint. The two connections involve different endpoints and trust relationships, so one does not automatically establish the other’s protection.',['Tunnel ends at gateway','Separate onward span','Independent HTTPS protection']),
 Q('Calculate','Using these two routes, which path carries 10.20.4.8 and which carries ordinary public browsing?','Explain how the VPN can be connected while some traffic remains direct.', '10.20.4.8 matches the /16 tunnel route. A public destination without another more specific route follows the ordinary default via the home gateway. That is a split arrangement. DNS and other exceptions must be checked separately in a real configuration.',['Internal prefix matches tunnel','Other destinations use default','Routes and DNS matter'])
 ],['Match the attempted flow against policy and state.','Identify the enforcement point.','Choose tunnel versus direct route by destination prefix.','Draw VPN and TLS protection as separate spans.'], 'A connected VPN does not imply every route enters it, and an allowed encrypted port is not a certificate of safe application behavior.')
 ,captures:C('A trace reaches a TLS alert after DNS and TCP succeed. Identify the last confirmed stage without inventing a certificate diagnosis the evidence does not contain.',[
 S('Read the events before guessing the cause',`Start with the observation point: this authored timeline is from the laptop’s interface after recording begins. A DNS answer supplies a candidate address. SYN, SYN-ACK and ACK establish the conventional TCP opening with a responder. ClientHello starts TLS negotiation, after which an alert is observed.

The trace confirms more than “nothing works.” It also confirms less than “the certificate is bad.” The alert’s code, protocol stage and context must be inspected to narrow TLS failure. No successful HTTP exchange is shown here. Listing the last positively confirmed stage gives you a useful boundary for the next test without pretending that one high-level symptom supplies a complete root cause.`,
 'Capture point: laptop interface, start t=0\n0.000 DNS answer: A 198.51.100.20\n0.010 → SYN to :443\n0.030 ← SYN,ACK\n0.031 → ACK\n0.040 → TLS ClientHello\n0.070 ← TLS alert (inspect details)',[['Confirmed TCP stage','Both directions participated in the opening.'],['TLS alert','Requires its actual details; a certificate cause is not established here.']]),
 S('Make a filter an explicit part of the observation',`A Wireshark display filter such as tcp narrows what you see from recorded packets. It can hide ordinary DNS or ARP packets that were captured. A capture filter limits what is recorded in the first place and uses different syntax.

Before saying “there was no DNS,” inspect both filters, interface choice and start time. Name data could also be cached or obtained over an encrypted resolver transport that does not appear as ordinary DNS decoding. The absence of an event from one filtered view can have several explanations. Record the view and collection conditions alongside your conclusion, especially when sharing evidence with someone who did not collect it.`,
 'Display filter tcp → ordinary DNS/ARP may be hidden\nCapture started after lookup → lookup never recorded\nCached name result → no new query required\nEncrypted DNS → visibility depends on transport/decoding',[['Display filter','Changes the view, not necessarily the recorded data.'],['Capture conditions','Define what absence can legitimately mean.']]),
 S('Compare viewpoints before comparing fields',`The laptop may show source 192.168.1.10 while a remote capture shows 203.0.113.5 because NAT rewrote the flow between them. If a reverse proxy terminates the connection, the origin can instead see a separate proxy-to-origin transport session.

Do not align two captures by assuming every address, port and sequence number must be identical across either kind of boundary. Label where each trace was collected and whether the traffic is translated or re-originated. Correlation needs appropriate endpoint context, timing and request identifiers. TLS also normally hides HTTP content from an ordinary passive view unless suitable secrets or endpoint instrumentation are available. Missing readable GET text is not proof that no request occurred.`,
 'Laptop view: private source address\nAfter NAT: translated public source\nAfter terminating proxy: possibly separate upstream connection\nEncrypted HTTP body is not ordinary plaintext evidence',[['NAT','Can alter addresses/ports within forwarded traffic.'],['Proxy termination','Can create different connection state entirely.']]),
 S('Treat analysis labels as hypotheses to corroborate',`A “retransmission” annotation is a tool’s interpretation of observed sequence patterns. It is not direct knowledge of why the sender sent again. Capture loss, hardware offloading and vantage point can make local traces look unusual.

Check relevant sequence ranges, acknowledgements, timing and endpoint logs before declaring path loss or an attack. A checksum warning in a local outgoing capture, for example, can depend on where hardware completion occurs relative to collection. The habit is to separate recorded bytes and events from inferred explanation. For practice, collect only traffic from your systems or a lab where you are authorized to observe it. Good investigation needs reliable provenance as well as a plausible story.`,
 'Observed: sequence/timing pattern\nTool annotation: possible retransmission\nExplanation: confirm with ACKs, another viewpoint or endpoint logs',[['Observation','What the collection actually records.'],['Interpretation','A conclusion that may need corroboration.']])
 ],[
 Q('Diagnose','What stages succeeded before the alert, and what cause remains unproven?','State the narrow conclusion justified by this DNS → TCP → TLS timeline.', 'A DNS answer was observed and TCP opening completed with the responding endpoint. TLS then produced an alert requiring its code and context. There is no shown successful HTTP exchange, and the trace alone does not establish a certificate-specific cause.',['DNS response observed','TCP opening completed','TLS details and root cause remain to inspect']),
 Q('Investigate','Your tcp-filtered view shows no DNS. What checks come before declaring that no lookup happened?','Give plausible reasons a lookup would be absent from this view.', 'Check display and capture filters, start time and interface. Also consider a cached answer or encrypted resolver transport. The view may hide packets, recording may miss them, or no new query may be needed. Explain the collection limits before inferring an absent operation.',['Filters/timing/interface','Cached state','Encrypted transport visibility']),
 Q('Explain','Why can laptop and origin-side traces show different client addresses or transport state?','How would NAT and proxy termination change the evidence at two capture points?', 'NAT can rewrite source fields. A terminating proxy can create a separate upstream connection with its own endpoints and sequence state. Label the viewpoints and connection boundaries before correlating observations; they need not describe unchanged end-to-end packet fields.',['Translation','Separate proxy connection','Labeled vantage points'])
 ],['Record the collection point and filters.','Order positive observations by protocol stage.','Separate recorded fields from inferred causes.','Choose the next test from the last confirmed stage.'], 'A capture is a bounded observation from a particular place and time. A missing event or an analysis label is not automatically a unique diagnosis.'),
 spoofing:C('A local cache suddenly maps the gateway to a different MAC, while a forged DNS answer names an unwanted endpoint. Identify the two different claims being trusted.',[
 S('Name the local claim that changed',`The laptop previously associated gateway .1 with MAC …:01. A new accepted claim associates it with …:99. That change affects which local interface receives frames for the selected next hop. It is an ARP-poisoning scenario if the mapping is misleading, rather than a new DNS answer or a change to the intended HTTP resource.

Basic ARP does not cryptographically authenticate ownership of the mapping. Depending on host behavior, topology and defenses, a malicious local participant may influence it. The immediate outcome can be disruption or redirection. Continuing transparent interception may require forwarding onward, and observing a changed entry alone does not prove every later application secret was exposed. Identify the altered state first.`,
 'Before: 192.168.1.1 → 02:00:00:00:00:01\nAfter:  192.168.1.1 → 02:00:00:00:00:99\nAffected choice: local frame recipient for the gateway',[['Forged claim','Local IP-to-link-address ownership.'],['Scope','The local neighbor information used for first-hop delivery.']]),
 S('Separate a forged name answer from the TLS identity',`Now a false DNS result directs accounts.example to an attacker-controlled address. The client can reach that address and still expect a TLS identity valid for accounts.example. Correct hostname, certificate-chain and key-possession checks remain required.

The attacker’s control over routing or a name answer does not itself supply the intended server’s private key or a valid trust path. Bypassing warnings, compromising a trusted issuer or altering the endpoint trust store would introduce additional failures, but they are not implied merely by the redirection. Distinguish where traffic is sent from which peer it successfully authenticates. That is why defenses can remain useful even after an earlier dependency is subverted.`,
 'False lookup: accounts.example → unwanted address\nBrowser expected TLS name: accounts.example\nAttacker must still satisfy that identity check to impersonate it',[['DNS target','Controls address selection in this scenario.'],['TLS identity','An independent claim with its own proof and trust rules.']]),
 S('Explain why source spoofing does not automatically provide replies',`An off-path sender can present another machine’s source IP if its environment permits forging that field. Ordinary reply routing follows the claimed source, so the response may go to the impersonated address rather than the actual sender.

Completing a TCP exchange requires dealing with response information and correct sequence state; authenticating a protected application adds further requirements. Sending a forged packet, observing a victim’s traffic and fully impersonating an authenticated endpoint are distinct capabilities. This distinction prevents the common leap from “IP addresses can be spoofed” to “anyone can trivially become any connected user.” Determine what the attacker can send, receive, observe and prove in the particular topology.`,
 'Actual sender X forges source Y\nResponder sends replies toward Y\nX may not observe them\nTwo-way state and authentication need more capability',[['Claimed source','A field used for reply routing, not proof of sender identity.'],['Off-path limitation','Sending does not automatically reveal the response.']]),
 S('Match each defense to the state it protects',`Local switch protections and segmentation can reduce opportunities to poison neighbor information. DNSSEC validation can authenticate signed DNS data. TLS authenticates its endpoint under the configured trust model. Source-validation practices can limit some forged-source traffic.

These mechanisms do not all protect the same thing. A TLS check can reject service impersonation without fixing a poisoned local cache that causes denial of service. DNSSEC does not encrypt the later HTTP response. A useful explanation identifies the forged claim, the component accepting it, the resulting traffic change and any independent protection still present. “Man in the middle” names a position or outcome, not the complete mechanism.`,
 'Neighbor mapping → local-layer protections\nSigned DNS data → DNSSEC validation\nTLS service identity → certificate + key proof\nEach control protects a different claim',[['Defense scope','Must match the claim being forged.'],['Remaining failure','An identity defense can coexist with disrupted availability.']])
 ],[
 Q('Security','Why should false DNS alone not normally impersonate accounts.example under correct HTTPS validation?','What additional capability is missing after redirecting this lookup?', 'The browser still expects a valid identity for accounts.example through certificate names, accepted chain and handshake key proof. The unwanted address alone supplies none of those capabilities. Additional trust or validation compromise would be a separate failure.',['Intended hostname','Accepted certificate/key proof','Redirection alone insufficient']),
 Q('Reason','Where do replies go when an off-path sender forges source Y, and why does TCP impersonation need more?','Distinguish injecting a forged packet from completing a two-way authenticated conversation.', 'Replies ordinarily route toward Y. The actual sender may not see them, while TCP state and later authentication require additional information or capabilities. Sending one forged field does not establish full session control. Assess the actual topology and attacker visibility.',['Replies follow claimed source','Response/state requirements','Authentication adds constraints']),
 Q('Distinguish','Which mapping changes in the ARP case, and which information changes in the false-DNS case?','Explain how the two poisonings influence different decisions in one web request.', 'ARP poisoning alters local IPv4-to-link-address information and the selected frame recipient. DNS poisoning alters name-resolution data and the endpoint address chosen for a name. They affect different state and scope, with different relevant protections.',['ARP local mapping','DNS name-related data','Different decisions and defenses'])
 ],['Identify the exact forged claim.','Locate the component that accepts it.','Trace the resulting delivery or address choice.','Check what independent authentication still requires.'], 'Moving traffic through an unwanted endpoint is not itself proof of decrypted TLS or successful impersonation of the intended secure service.'),
 availability:C('The server CPU is idle while users cannot connect, but another outage occurs at low bandwidth. Find the exhausted resource before buying more workers.',[
 S('Locate a saturated link before looking inside the application',`The access link can carry 100 Mb/s while unwanted traffic offered toward it exceeds that capacity. Legitimate packets compete at the bottleneck or are dropped before useful delivery. The server can have spare CPU because many requests never reach application processing.

Adding application workers does not increase this link’s capacity. A host rule dropping packets can save host processing after arrival, but traffic has already consumed the access segment. This is a placement problem as much as a volume problem: protection for the link must act before that constrained segment or distribute traffic so it avoids the same bottleneck. A quiet CPU graph cannot by itself establish a healthy user path.`,
 'Access capacity: 100 Mb/s\nOffered unwanted traffic: exceeds link capacity\nServer CPU: mostly idle\nUsers: timeouts / unusable service',[['Bottleneck','The path resource, not necessarily application compute.'],['Host filtering','Can act only after traffic has crossed the upstream segment.']]),
 S('Calculate an application bottleneck at low traffic volume',`Now consider a different service with 20 workers. Each expensive request occupies one worker for about two seconds. In this simplified steady-state model, capacity is about 20/2 = 10 requests per second. An arrival rate of 15 such requests per second can build a queue even with little bandwidth use.

Request count alone also hides cost variation: a cheap cached read and an expensive database operation are not equal work. CPU, memory, connection pools, downstream quotas or database capacity can constrain service. Measure the actual resource and service time rather than applying a bandwidth remedy to an application-work problem. The arithmetic is a model for reasoning, not a performance promise for a real deployment.`,
 '20 workers × one job each\nMean occupancy: 2 seconds per job\nToy capacity: 10 jobs/s\nArrival: 15 expensive jobs/s → backlog pressure',[['Service capacity','Depends on occupancy, not just packet volume.'],['Growing work','An unbounded queue can consume further resources.']]),
 S('Understand reflection by its false source claim and asymmetry',`Some UDP services send a response to the source address presented in a request. If a sender can forge that source and the response is larger, the service can be induced to send reflected traffic toward another endpoint. Distribution across sources or infrastructure can compound availability pressure.

For the illustrative payload sizes, 3000/60 gives a 50-to-one response/request ratio. Actual on-wire ratios include protocol overhead and depend on the service. The important properties are the unverified return-address claim and the asymmetric resource cost. You can reason about responsible service design, source validation and filtering without generating an attack or scanning other people’s systems.`,
 'Illustrative request payload: 60 bytes\nIllustrative response payload: 3000 bytes\nPayload-only ratio: 50×\nReply destination follows the claimed source',[['Reflection','Response sent toward the presented source address.'],['Amplification','Output or resource cost can exceed the triggering input.']]),
 S('Choose a control before the resource is consumed',`Upstream filtering or distributed capacity can help a link bottleneck. Work budgets, caching, request-cost controls and suitable rate limits can address application bottlenecks. Connection-state protection addresses another resource again. A control should reject or reduce unwanted cost before doing the expensive work it is meant to protect.

Legitimate demand can resemble harmful demand, so verify useful service for real users rather than only a lower packet counter. Distributed denial of service is an availability problem involving multiple sources or infrastructure; naming it does not identify the exact exhausted resource. Start with the bottleneck and observation point, then justify the placement and tradeoffs of a control.`,
 'Link saturation → act upstream / distribute before the segment\nExpensive API work → bound cost before backend execution\nConnection state → protect the stateful admission point',[['Before the bottleneck','Reduces the resource cost that matters.'],['Useful service','The outcome to preserve, not just a reduced counter.']])
 ],[
 Q('Diagnose','Why would adding workers not fix the 100 Mb/s access-link outage?','What resource is exhausted while CPU remains idle in this case?', 'The constrained resource is upstream bandwidth. More workers cannot remove traffic already occupying that link or increase its capacity. Mitigation needs relevant upstream action or distribution before the bottleneck, rather than a compute-only change.',['Link bandwidth','Workers do not add capacity','Protection before bottleneck']),
 Q('Explain','Why can a host drop rule save CPU but fail to restore that access link?','At which side of the bottleneck must bandwidth protection act?', 'The packets crossed the access link before reaching the host rule. Discarding there can reduce host work but cannot reclaim consumed segment capacity. To protect the link, unwanted traffic must be reduced or absorbed before it traverses that segment.',['Traffic already crossed link','Host cost differs from link cost','Upstream placement']),
 Q('Calculate','With 20 workers and two seconds per expensive request, why can 15 requests/s overload this toy service?','What does this low-bandwidth example show about counting requests without their cost?', 'The simplified capacity is about 10 requests/s, below the 15/s arrival rate, so pending work grows unless admission or workload changes. Cost, occupancy and constrained resource matter even when bandwidth is low. Real systems require measurement beyond this simplified model.',['About 10 jobs/s capacity','Arrival exceeds service capacity','Request cost/resource measurement'])
 ],['Locate the constrained resource from evidence.','Calculate whether offered work exceeds its capacity.','Reduce unwanted cost before that resource is consumed.','Verify legitimate service rather than optimizing only a traffic count.'], 'A CPU graph, request count or attack label alone does not locate an availability bottleneck. Control placement must protect the resource actually running out.'),
 capstone:C('A user cannot open a report. One attempt gets a DNS answer and a certificate mismatch; another receives an HTTP 502. Build two different investigations from the actual evidence.',[
 S('Declare the path you are explaining',`For the first attempt, assume a fresh HTTPS-over-TCP request with usable local configuration and no reusable connection. The DNS query itself needs a route and local delivery to the configured resolver. An answer selects a candidate address; it is not yet proof of web-service reachability.

The laptop then selects a route toward that candidate, resolves the local next hop if necessary and sends. IPv4 NAT may rewrite the source at a boundary. TCP opens, TLS validates the intended identity, and only a successful protected channel can carry the intended HTTP exchange. These dependencies can be reused, cached or arranged differently in a real browser, so the assumptions make this particular explanation testable.`,
 'URL: https://reports.example/monthly\nConfigured resolver supplies A 198.51.100.20\nTCP opening to selected endpoint completes\nTLS result: hostname mismatch\nSuccessful HTTP report response: not shown',[['DNS dependency','Already requires routes and local delivery.'],['Last confirmed stage','TCP completed before this stated identity error.']]),
 S('Investigate the name mismatch without turning off validation',`Compare reports.example from the URL with the certificate names presented by the reached endpoint. Then inspect the resolved address, proxy configuration and virtual-host selection. A stale or unwanted DNS result might reach the wrong service, but certificate deployment or proxy routing could also be wrong.

Choose a test that separates those hypotheses while preserving the intended hostname. Replacing the URL with a bare IP or bypassing the warning changes the identity check and can hide the cause. The successful TCP opening already narrows the failure: repeating generic Wi-Fi resets adds less information than examining the exact identity mismatch at the stage that reported it. State remaining uncertainty instead of declaring a DNS cause from the certificate error alone.`,
 'Hypothesis A: wrong endpoint selected\nHypothesis B: right endpoint, wrong certificate/hosting config\nCompare URL name + certificate + resolved/reached endpoint',[['Discriminating test','Needs to separate A from B, not merely make the warning disappear.'],['Preserved intent','Keep the expected service identity in the test.']]),
 S('Start a different investigation when HTTP actually answers',`On a second attempt, the user receives a genuine HTTPS 502 from the public edge. That response establishes that an HTTP responder was reached over a sufficiently working secure connection. It points toward an upstream-serving problem reported by the edge, not the earlier pre-HTTP identity failure.

Inspect edge-to-origin connection attempts, TLS, timeouts and backend responses. The origin application might never have seen the request. A cached page could still work during this failure, so ask which resource and serving path failed. The two attempts can share a user complaint while needing different tests. Progress comes from the evidence boundary, not from one memorized troubleshooting checklist applied identically to every browser error.`,
 'Second attempt: browser receives HTTPS 502 from edge\nClient → edge exchange worked sufficiently\nEdge → origin leg needs investigation\nOrigin application receipt remains unproven',[['New positive evidence','An HTTP response exists now.'],['Serving boundary','The error can be generated by an intermediary.']]),
 S('Write a report someone else could test',`A useful report records exact URL, time, client path, observation point and result. Then it states the last confirmed dependency, leading hypotheses and one next test with predicted outcomes. Include return routes, filtering and cached or reused state when relevant.

For this case, “Wi-Fi connected; Internet broken” is too vague. “TCP opening to the selected endpoint succeeds; certificate names do not cover reports.example” is actionable. For the second case, “edge returned 502; correlate its upstream attempt” leads to a different action. A full page can also involve multiple origins and connections. Keep claims scoped to the exchange observed while using the overall model to decide where more evidence would matter.`,
 'Observation → bounded conclusion\nHypothesis → test → expected alternative outcomes\nRecord vantage point, time and relevant state\nRevise the explanation when new evidence changes the boundary',[['Reproducible evidence','Lets another person check the same claim.'],['One useful next test','Separates causes instead of changing everything at once.']])
 ],[
 Q('Synthesize','Walk through a fresh HTTPS-over-TCP request, including the networking needed by DNS itself.','Explain the dependencies before an HTTP report can arrive, stating the fresh-connection assumptions.', 'Reach the resolver using local configuration, route and neighbor delivery. Resolve the name, choose an address and route, resolve its local next hop as needed and account for translation if present. Establish TCP, validate TLS and exchange HTTP. Caches, reuse, proxies and other transports can change the observed sequence.',['DNS also needs route/link delivery','Address, next hop and possible translation','TCP/TLS/HTTP under stated assumptions']),
 Q('Investigate','Which comparison separates wrong endpoint selection from wrong certificate configuration in the first attempt?','What evidence should follow the successful TCP opening and hostname mismatch?', 'Compare intended URL name, certificate coverage and chain, reached address and relevant proxy/hosting selection. Keep identity validation intact. A DNS answer can be wrong, but the mismatch alone does not uniquely establish DNS as the cause. Choose a test with different expected outcomes for the two hypotheses.',['Expected versus certified identity','Endpoint/proxy context','Discriminate causes without bypassing validation']),
 Q('Diagnose','Why should the second attempt’s 502 lead to a different next test than the first attempt’s TLS mismatch?','Turn these two “cannot open report” complaints into separately bounded investigations.', 'The first attempt fails at reported TLS identity validation before a shown HTTP response. The second reaches an HTTP edge responder that reports an upstream problem. Investigate the first name/endpoint/certificate comparison and the second origin-serving leg, rather than treating both as undifferentiated network failure.',['Different confirmed stages','Identity comparison versus upstream leg','Narrow evidence and next test'])
 ],['State the request and starting assumptions.','Draw dependencies and actual connection boundaries.','Locate the last confirmed stage in each attempt.','Choose a test that distinguishes the remaining hypotheses and record its predicted meaning.'], 'The same user-visible complaint can arise before HTTP or from a real HTTP responder. Diagnose the observed exchange, not the generic wording of the complaint.')
};
